#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338901 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105734 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9331987e-8676-5bdb-b4ce-2129b1b09a8c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Altitude of a Triangle Given Its Vertices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "093467b9-0067-542c-b65c-633c1cfc4b53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336113} {:answer/id #uuid "3859e15a-56e5-569e-825a-f822ed3eac71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{6}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336557} {:answer/id #uuid "6c2eb1b1-c7b8-5381-b418-d9244be8d489" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336991} {:answer/id #uuid "d8d294a1-32a0-5b4f-94b7-ee30ffc9011a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337918} {:answer/id #uuid "de85db2c-4065-5f60-92b7-7fdad6d31158" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337972}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8d294a1-32a0-5b4f-94b7-ee30ffc9011a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337918} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d1b4065-470d-599f-81c3-c9f0fd87b16f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338536}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9999542b-9f9f-5d86-95f6-bcd8d4190efb" :question/math-academy-id "q-71116" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $ZH$ of the $△\\,WYZ$, if $W(2,3,2)$, $Y(1,2,1)$, $Z(1,-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $ZH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,WYZ$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}ZH\\cdot WY\n$$\n\nSo, to find $ZH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{WY}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{WZ}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{WY} & =y-w \\\\  & =(i+2j+k)-(2i+3j+2k) \\\\  & =-i-j-k \\\\  & =⟨-1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{WZ} & =z-w \\\\  & =(i-j+k)-(2i+3j+2k) \\\\  & =-i-4j-k \\\\  & =⟨-1,-4,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{WY}\\times \\overset{\\to }{WZ} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -4 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot (-1)-(-4)\\cdot (-1))i-((-1)\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot (-1))j+((-1)\\cdot (-4)-(-1)\\cdot (-1))k \\\\  & =-3i+3k \\\\  & =⟨-3,0,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{WY}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{WZ}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{WY}\\times \\overset{\\to }{WZ}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,WYZ$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $WY$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}WY & =|\\overset{\\to }{WY}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}ZH\\cdot WY,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}ZH\\cdot WY \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}ZH\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2} & =ZH\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ ZH & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ ZH & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338904 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105734 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9331987e-8676-5bdb-b4ce-2129b1b09a8c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Altitude of a Triangle Given Its Vertices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f3c7d45-2508-558a-a671-704a2f709aa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336627} {:answer/id #uuid "733fefab-043d-5fe4-93ed-cf5c6a3298cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337070} {:answer/id #uuid "ca0454d2-6cd6-50e2-9a7f-17ac15f3a137" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337812} {:answer/id #uuid "d3fb8394-0a4f-5554-bef2-b4565678aeac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337886} {:answer/id #uuid "f7adc41f-f450-52b1-9898-009b7b23875a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338203}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3fb8394-0a4f-5554-bef2-b4565678aeac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337886} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1e3142c-b7af-51c6-a353-db8d28d28403" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338622}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6da0a984-327a-597f-9055-73fa298d90dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-71128" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $DH$ of the $△\\,DMA$, if $D(-1,2,1)$, $M(-1,0,-1)$, $A(1,-2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $DH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,DMA$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}DH\\cdot MA\n$$\n\nSo, to find $DH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{MD}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{MA}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{MD} & =d-m \\\\  & =(-i+2j+k)-(-i-k) \\\\  & =2j+2k \\\\  & =⟨0,2,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{MA} & =a-m \\\\  & =(i-2j+k)-(-i-k) \\\\  & =2i-2j+2k \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{MD}\\times \\overset{\\to }{MA} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 2-(-2)\\cdot 2)i-(0\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 2)j+(0\\cdot (-2)-2\\cdot 2)k \\\\  & =8i+4j-4k \\\\  & =⟨8,4,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{MD}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{MA}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{MD}\\times \\overset{\\to }{MA}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{8}^{2}+{4}^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{96} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,DMA$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{4\\sqrt{6}}{2}=2\\sqrt{6}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $MA$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}MA & =|\\overset{\\to }{MA}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}DH\\cdot MA,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}DH\\cdot MA \\\\ 2\\sqrt{6} & =\\frac{1}{2}DH\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{6} & =DH\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ DH & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ DH & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176559 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175952 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "be9ac99c-424e-5e90-b26f-c26e4da704d7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Final Position of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62be3c05-a4ae-5efb-9948-679341242985" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "92" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336919} {:answer/id #uuid "8405c10d-b8b6-5c71-8189-17737f465d6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "105" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337224} {:answer/id #uuid "88db8cb5-299e-5069-9bcf-7be99175abe6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337266} {:answer/id #uuid "8a915290-5bd6-518d-95de-cb51f1f093a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "97" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337282} {:answer/id #uuid "bea68b0e-1b25-5071-ad8e-8a44fba3ec1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "93" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337727}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8405c10d-b8b6-5c71-8189-17737f465d6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "105" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337224} :answer-field/id #uuid "740967d6-ac89-5f9b-b1f3-bcaa5e18f367" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338475}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f38c2d58-f071-5d09-92ca-36e11adfb46f" :question/math-academy-id "q-7113" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t>0$ is given by $v(t)=4t+3{t}^{2}.$ At time $t=1$ the particle is located at the position $x=12.$ What is the position of the particle at time $t=4?$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The position of the particle at $t=4$ is equal to its position at $t=1$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [1,4].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(4)=x(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{4}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nCarrying out the computations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(4) & =12+{\\int }_{1}^{4}(4t+3{t}^{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =12+[2{t}^{2}+{t}^{3}]_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =12+([2(4)^{2}+{4}^{3}]-[2(1)^{2}+{1}^{3}]) \\\\  & =12+([32+64]-[2+1]) \\\\  & =12+(96-3) \\\\  & =12+93 \\\\  & =105\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the position of the particle at $t=4$ is $x=105.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393091 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119781 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ecdc6a5a-5aba-5547-97aa-db3cd2e90ae6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Minor in a NxN Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fdecdb2a-be1a-535a-bee9-9659ab7d1d37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}-7 & 3 & \\,1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & \\,2 \\\\ 11 & 3 & \\,-3\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393093} {:answer/id #uuid "81a8a04c-db9c-57ca-b056-75bc4bda4bb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}-7 & 0 & \\,1 \\\\ 10 & -1 & \\,0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & \\,2\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393094} {:answer/id #uuid "07c43492-f6dd-5136-82a3-13dad2882b0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}-7 & 0 & \\,1 \\\\ 10 & -1 & \\,0 \\\\ 11 & 0 & \\,-3\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393095} {:answer/id #uuid "01e785ae-c084-571e-93e7-3cc45466fd54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & \\,1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & \\,0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & \\,2\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393096} {:answer/id #uuid "435c0dce-546b-54b9-bdab-d4baeb3c9ec3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}10 & 1 & \\,0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & \\,2 \\\\ 11 & 3 & \\,-3\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393097}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fdecdb2a-be1a-535a-bee9-9659ab7d1d37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}-7 & 3 & \\,1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & \\,2 \\\\ 11 & 3 & \\,-3\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393093} :answer-field/id #uuid "b15b527c-7c8f-54df-aefc-bcdbdc70d5ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393092}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "caf1e0fb-eb87-5e71-a542-0c1755eff5ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-71189" :question/problem "What is the minor of ${p}_{23}$ in  $P=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 3 & 0 & \\,1 \\\\ 10 & 1 & -1 & \\,0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 3 & \\,2 \\\\ 11 & 3 & 0 & \\,-3\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "To get the minor of ${p}_{23}$, we remove the  $2$nd row and the $3$rd column:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 3 & ∗ & 1 \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & -1 & ∗ \\\\ -2 & 0 & ∗ & 2 \\\\ 11 & 3 & ∗ & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the minor is \n\n$$\n{M}_{23}=|\\begin{aligned}-7 & 3 & \\,1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & \\,2 \\\\ 11 & 3 & \\,-3\\end{aligned}|.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393098 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119781 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ecdc6a5a-5aba-5547-97aa-db3cd2e90ae6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Minor in a NxN Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ecf02146-e1b0-5539-b4e5-5e4db585f712" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 9 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393100} {:answer/id #uuid "639ab66c-d0ba-5e67-b27c-fee7c12393fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 7 & 10 & 8\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393101} {:answer/id #uuid "e0e8b3c9-465c-55d4-b781-46d61c898c4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 7 & 2 & 8\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393102} {:answer/id #uuid "e9c7b7b0-99e1-5530-8565-49e93df10d3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 10 & 8\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393103} {:answer/id #uuid "923bf90c-f200-5ae2-850a-6b9cff1e3bf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 10 & 2 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393104}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecf02146-e1b0-5539-b4e5-5e4db585f712" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 9 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393100} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd952f15-a2c4-53c4-ab18-fdcc2715d46f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393099}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c5a3f02c-617a-514d-b9f7-86ea907e6206" :question/math-academy-id "q-71190" :question/problem "What is the minor of ${u}_{34}$ in  $U=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 7 & 10 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 9 & -3 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "To get the minor of ${u}_{34}$, we remove the  $3$rd row and the $4$th column:\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 1 & ∗ \\\\ 5 & -1 & 0 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & ∗ & 8 \\\\ 9 & -3 & 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the minor is \n\n$$\n{M}_{34}=|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 9 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}|.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393112 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115391 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b19f5f01-948e-5f44-8016-63073ed31dbc" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Scalar Function Multiple Rule for Differentiation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9538c922-626d-57e3-875e-1c3a6b6e5675" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}(4{t}^{2}+t){e}^{t} \\\\ ({t}^{2}-1){e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393114} {:answer/id #uuid "8c39359a-bdf1-5f7a-abe2-7ec39aa9faf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}(2{t}^{3}+6{t}^{2}-3){e}^{t} \\\\ ({t}^{2}+2t+1){e}^{t} \\\\ (4t-3){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393115} {:answer/id #uuid "cd118cf1-a60f-5278-b6b0-774581fb4af6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}(-5{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+1){e}^{t} \\\\ (3{t}^{2}-t-1){e}^{t} \\\\ (t+2){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393116} {:answer/id #uuid "0231d3d9-379e-569f-8987-f29126252cf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}({t}^{2}-4t+4) \\\\ ({t}^{2}-1) \\\\ (2t+3)\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393117} {:answer/id #uuid "52e3e13a-2c9a-5dea-92ad-1d1be14d9577" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7{t}^{3}{e}^{t} \\\\ {t}^{2}{e}^{t} \\\\ 4t{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393118}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c39359a-bdf1-5f7a-abe2-7ec39aa9faf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}(2{t}^{3}+6{t}^{2}-3){e}^{t} \\\\ ({t}^{2}+2t+1){e}^{t} \\\\ (4t-3){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393115} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5faccf8-2ac3-56c6-9185-9f48d31adb8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393113}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1c08c320-55ed-59e1-87a0-c92a722748de" :question/math-academy-id "q-71233" :question/problem "Find ${(u(t)f(t))}^{′}$ where $f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{3}-3 \\\\ {t}^{2}+1 \\\\ 4t-7\\end{aligned}]$ and $u(t)={e}^{t}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar function multiple rule, we can write\n\n$$\n{(u(t)f(t))}^{′}={u}^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t){f}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's find ${u}^{′}(t)$ and ${f}^{′}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}-3) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t-7)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6{t}^{2} \\\\ 2t \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u(t)f(t))}^{′} & ={u}^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t){f}^{′}(t) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{3}-3 \\\\ {t}^{2}+1 \\\\ 4t-7\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}6{t}^{2} \\\\ 2t \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(2{t}^{3}-3){e}^{t} \\\\ ({t}^{2}+1){e}^{t} \\\\ (4t-7){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}6{t}^{2}{e}^{t} \\\\ 2t{e}^{t} \\\\ 4{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(2{t}^{3}+6{t}^{2}-3){e}^{t} \\\\ ({t}^{2}+2t+1){e}^{t} \\\\ (4t-3){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403461 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068900 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "66ade855-4b16-514b-b44a-1d6b9d61ef28" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift Applied to an Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8efb215-a4e3-57a5-9c28-aeceec68ab3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403463} {:answer/id #uuid "dc7c1d24-77af-5b22-8748-eb78e3181bee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403464} {:answer/id #uuid "ac8a280a-f3e5-57ca-a313-b4a7d3f28178" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403465} {:answer/id #uuid "2e259bca-b084-538c-98c1-c8f9fb1db908" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403466} {:answer/id #uuid "54400882-3ae8-5fef-9856-f37d1e3a6878" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403467}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8efb215-a4e3-57a5-9c28-aeceec68ab3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403463} :answer-field/id #uuid "153448b6-9533-5575-9ff3-816d932b2ca7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403462}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d28d182e-64fa-5ae5-be71-7e9fd891464e" :question/math-academy-id "q-7124" :question/problem "![](images/1a/1a54cae748796775b76ade4d9060d4eafe6bb84fd45ce725bd516a7db2681ba9.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}+c.$ Determine the value of $c$ and find the equation of the curve." :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of $y={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}$ is $y=0.$ \n\nThe vertical translation $y={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}+c$ shifts the asymptote to $y=c.$\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=2.$ Therefore, $c=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366219 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147427 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a19140af-1870-5dee-86a2-6052451803d4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab715e39-7262-53f8-a589-7932e5948409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366221} {:answer/id #uuid "4df91f2a-726d-5a75-89e2-fbf683b5f3e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366222} {:answer/id #uuid "15089811-be63-5322-bff3-9d9751b255f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366223} {:answer/id #uuid "73dbba28-230f-587a-b4f0-389b59e14572" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366224} {:answer/id #uuid "d97b5ca9-cd86-5251-bc55-f166bb43da71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366225}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15089811-be63-5322-bff3-9d9751b255f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366223} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a7171c3-579f-5a05-8896-bebb6c2c2177" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366220}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "96c9a429-d310-55eb-845b-95fc86ca78d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-71448" :question/problem "Given the two planes $x-2y+z=0$ and $x-y+1=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The normal vectors of the planes are parallel.\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $0.$\n- The planes intersect on a line." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x-2y+z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,-2,1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x-y+1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,-1,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,-2,1⟩\\times ⟨1,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =i+j+k \\\\  & =⟨1,1,1⟩\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338945 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092193 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ed5facf-f654-5890-974f-bbdcd9641515" :knowledge-point/title "Applying De Moivre's Theorem to Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ebbca7a-6aa7-54dc-9f74-c4ad0484ba9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )+2\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336172} {:answer/id #uuid "74e7e0a2-11e8-5bdf-9a4b-064f2cb3a59f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337085} {:answer/id #uuid "98a599e8-dc6d-5258-8147-8b55cbd27282" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+6\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337421} {:answer/id #uuid "c21c538f-aea8-54a9-a544-4b1ce34929b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337757} {:answer/id #uuid "d448d8a7-ba02-5464-ade6-1bd3866a205f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-3\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337887}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74e7e0a2-11e8-5bdf-9a4b-064f2cb3a59f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337085} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d6bb9fd-afac-592d-97a0-68e60889667e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338500}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6f604609-4011-5a69-9aea-4ff0b17e67cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-71452" :question/problem "Simplify the expression $\\frac{1}{{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )]}^{6}}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )]}^{6}}={[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )]}^{-6}.\n$$\n\nDe Moivre's Theorem states that\n\n$$\n{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\phi )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\phi )]}^{n}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\phi )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\phi ).\n$$\n\nIn our expression, we have $n=-6$ and $\\phi =\\theta .$ Substituting in these values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )]}^{6}} & ={[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )]}^{-6} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-6\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-6\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the fact that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\phi )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\phi )$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\phi )=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\phi )$ for any input $\\phi ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-6\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-6\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\theta ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338951 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147427 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a19140af-1870-5dee-86a2-6052451803d4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e2af9cd-f9db-5fc9-8508-c2ba1287e6d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336616} {:answer/id #uuid "50c7c369-3431-583c-b3ba-dcf5cf8e6396" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336772} {:answer/id #uuid "797b27ec-b464-5a02-98e5-4ee61ae53020" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337124} {:answer/id #uuid "9be98a9a-295e-5a46-8190-87c5fa70b702" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337445} {:answer/id #uuid "f6c17eec-564b-5903-b73e-2e8ff9b63df5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338197}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e2af9cd-f9db-5fc9-8508-c2ba1287e6d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336616} :answer-field/id #uuid "f48d4703-a664-5aa9-a3f6-2820a939fe7d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338704}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eb6d9712-df0c-5f4b-a820-ccc73d9a1ec9" :question/math-academy-id "q-71468" :question/problem "Given the two planes $2x-4y+6z+2=0$ and $-x+2y-3z=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $0.$\n- The planes intersect on a straight line.\n- The planes are coincident." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $2x-4y+6z+2=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,-4,6⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $-x+2y-3z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨-1,2,-3⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨2,-4,6⟩\\times ⟨-1,2,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -4 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =0i+0j+0k \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}=0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are parallel. Hence, the corresponding planes are also parallel. So, the planes can't intersect on a line.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed,  the second plane passes through the origin  $O(0,0,0)$ but the first plane doesn't: \n\n$$\n2(0)-4(0)+6(0)+2=2\\ne 0\n$$\n\n So, the planes can't be coincident.\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338954 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087859 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "30bb1401-f704-520c-a23e-5a238e44960e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Circle Given a Description"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f9ae437-1d85-50ec-958b-e8760b5b0e64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=125" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336328} {:answer/id #uuid "308f53c4-2ae7-55da-984b-1a65d78f95cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336497} {:answer/id #uuid "73254016-15bc-578d-98b1-e2110dfab2dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337068} {:answer/id #uuid "90833b0d-0556-5041-9d09-6af44e6c3d8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337342} {:answer/id #uuid "a58f261c-1656-5a28-9ad6-6ecc994c1001" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337535}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "308f53c4-2ae7-55da-984b-1a65d78f95cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336497} :answer-field/id #uuid "064b2f2d-b064-5112-8c60-48e6b84edd0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338286}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e777ee84-35c1-5291-bae1-8ca4f484b4c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-71506" :question/problem "Which equation describes the set of all points $(x,y)$ in the coordinate plane that are $5$ units away from the origin?" :question/worked-solution "The set of all points $(x,y)$ that are a distance $5$ from the origin defines a circle with radius $r=5$ centered at the origin, as shown below.\n\n![](images/39/398adead10233d1af61cd9aa44f14d51eba9c195d30c9d6b16015a3391403908.png)\n\nSo, the required equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={5}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083920 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083254 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "646467d1-f764-5a3c-b03b-7d74e588611d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Speed of a Particle at a Given Time"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28186592-08bc-549e-9c22-c79ee3ea6745" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336408} {:answer/id #uuid "3025f42b-c5db-534a-b7db-5eb1874e4c83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336493} {:answer/id #uuid "61f846a2-1971-5874-84e8-c682412f989c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336914} {:answer/id #uuid "7bbd7131-4a34-5bb1-b11e-243d31511be7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337150} {:answer/id #uuid "a724f86f-6e25-582e-8cc1-02e5086a17ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337545}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61f846a2-1971-5874-84e8-c682412f989c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336914} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f0ed294-f84f-5efe-be0c-4f49c2d7af7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338389}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6995c088-70db-5fcb-a926-3a5bb8ce6736" :question/math-academy-id "q-7151" :question/problem "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2},\\,3t+7⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the speed of the particle at time $t=4.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t+7)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=4,$ we substitute in the above:\n\n$$\nv=⟨4,3⟩\n$$\n\nTo find the speed at $t=4$ we calculate the magnitude of $v(4),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|v(4)| & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the speed when $t=4$ is $5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338957 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092384 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a05f6c0-98ab-5197-922e-8e8dff1d4302" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of a Normal Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "105a0ee2-ecfe-5f6b-abf3-c2b8990ff5e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336186} {:answer/id #uuid "8afbf511-93b1-574c-aea0-f719ed534072" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337288} {:answer/id #uuid "9b55a9ee-2b2e-5710-b96a-5529ce814f84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337440} {:answer/id #uuid "b83a05a7-995d-5078-bf9a-4b58abb10a90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337665} {:answer/id #uuid "e2cfabde-266f-53a5-a5bf-2a8c9659561a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338007}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "105a0ee2-ecfe-5f6b-abf3-c2b8990ff5e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336186} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb39a159-1af8-51a3-9955-b31d00f03bbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338721}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0b93f0c3-f4cf-5391-8374-d4b6970c54ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-71524" :question/problem "Find the slope of the normal line to the curve $y={x}^{2}-5$ at the point $(2,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)={x}^{2}-5.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-5)=2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{′}(2)=2(2)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088284 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087763 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9ec9e0af-bbdc-5a29-bdbb-602e4d1f016a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Acceleration Vector of a Particle Given Its Velocity Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94c87ef4-516b-5fd3-946b-8f9c62c98016" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a = ⟨5, 2t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086359} {:answer/id #uuid "9e58aea6-19dc-5488-bc80-93d7632e221f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a = ⟨ - 8, 4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086360} {:answer/id #uuid "39afe4ac-830b-58c4-b06b-d3fb4983f0bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a = ⟨5t, 2t^{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086361} {:answer/id #uuid "5f00365e-8d58-5b9f-b448-aa58586fbb31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a = ⟨5, 4t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086362} {:answer/id #uuid "6586d8bf-7a12-561b-af59-2bdb282646dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a = ⟨5, 2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086363}] :answer-field/id #uuid "c8eeedcb-7380-5490-8e80-bea8245eee54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086358} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ed876eb-e8b1-58fc-a7c7-49ce58aab2a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨-8,\\,4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393148} {:answer/id #uuid "6c9432b2-20cf-5e0d-b627-365bf8bb517b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨5t,\\,2{t}^{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393149} {:answer/id #uuid "5aeefe63-a288-5b47-85c7-d2a2775613d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨5,\\,2t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393150} {:answer/id #uuid "ee5fa715-a949-54b8-a77a-babd9c98996a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨5,\\,4t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393151} {:answer/id #uuid "5f894125-91dc-5e2c-bd9f-0d467b5542d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨5,\\,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393152}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee5fa715-a949-54b8-a77a-babd9c98996a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨5,\\,4t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393151} :answer-field/id #uuid "78dfd377-d2e6-5c1a-8aa3-e854870d70f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393147}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fca59699-e72b-5149-821a-1daf54baaef5" :question/math-academy-id "q-7156" :question/problem "The velocity vector $v=⟨u,v⟩$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nv=⟨5t-8,\\,2{t}^{2}+4⟩.\n$$\n\nCalculate the acceleration vector $a.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5t-8),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}+4)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5,\\,4t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393160 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087764 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "380357c3-4914-5137-9449-0e02076cb248" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Acceleration Vector of a Particle Given Its Position Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bb87f44-9e54-547c-bf05-ae2ff892adc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨4{t}^{3},\\,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393162} {:answer/id #uuid "9e4c5bec-76b9-51aa-955d-d6d64eddf07d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨6{t}^{3},\\,4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393163} {:answer/id #uuid "783f3cce-1dea-53dc-8a90-43631ed18539" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨{t}^{4},\\,3t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393164} {:answer/id #uuid "08c13741-b540-5324-ade7-9e8d1017f6ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨{t}^{3},\\,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393165} {:answer/id #uuid "beb86fd9-f93a-555f-a0ac-cfee71d85783" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨4{t}^{3},\\,6t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393166}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bb87f44-9e54-547c-bf05-ae2ff892adc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=⟨4{t}^{3},\\,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393162} :answer-field/id #uuid "f76bc762-0563-53c1-a43e-9693c4d41279" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393161}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "018374cc-40c9-5d0d-a1b9-641708c13290" :question/math-academy-id "q-7165" :question/problem "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5},\\,3{t}^{2}-1⟩.\n$$\n\nCalculate the acceleration vector $a.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we need to differentiate the velocity vector. So first, we need to compute the velocity vector, which we can do this by differentiating the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{4},\\,6t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4{t}^{3},\\,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393167 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105702 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0670172-77cb-5e88-b78d-e5136c06095f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Vector Projection Using Components"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b3a993e-29b5-59cb-99e5-ded901d8bd0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,0,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393169} {:answer/id #uuid "c7002ec2-9546-5f0e-821c-ebe64ba0bd6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{4}{19},\\frac{12}{19},-\\frac{12}{19}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393170} {:answer/id #uuid "7366fd93-b330-5afb-bffc-fc0a9d70dc12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{2}{5},-\\frac{1}{5}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393171} {:answer/id #uuid "3da3af95-6571-5bb0-ac07-5accef6e0a78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393172} {:answer/id #uuid "736a78cf-613a-5d25-a09d-d0901781721f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{3}{17},\\frac{1}{17},\\frac{1}{17}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393173}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7002ec2-9546-5f0e-821c-ebe64ba0bd6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{4}{19},\\frac{12}{19},-\\frac{12}{19}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393170} :answer-field/id #uuid "81ed75df-1ce1-5ebc-81a9-b722c6382e6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393168}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4a5f805d-9915-543e-b3c6-5d61cd49da57" :question/math-academy-id "q-71662" :question/problem "Let $c=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $d=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].$ Find the vector projection of $c$ onto $d.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the vector projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{d}\\,c & =\\frac{c\\cdot d}{d\\cdot d}d \\\\  & =\\frac{{c}_{x}{d}_{x}+{c}_{y}{d}_{y}+{c}_{z}{d}_{z}}{{d}_{x}^{2}+{d}_{y}^{2}+{d}_{z}^{2}}d \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 3+1\\cdot (-3)}{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-3)^{2}}d \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{19}⟨1,3,-3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{4}{19},\\frac{12}{19},-\\frac{12}{19}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338970 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064181 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9836abbe-e172-5c2f-8b19-145ef6d5c02d" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Cosecant Squared"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b98751b-5a6f-5701-a079-66efabd0c54e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3tan⁡y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336591} {:answer/id #uuid "6c064b7d-ae95-54c0-99a2-5b48ab4e6e2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3cot⁡y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336987} {:answer/id #uuid "898bc83c-2d65-5f25-8fe3-14152653f4f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3tan⁡y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337272} {:answer/id #uuid "bab226dc-1012-58ab-b0e7-c57b6031dacd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3cot⁡y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337686} {:answer/id #uuid "fe8ec3ab-b777-58b0-80b5-c49a97bea68f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3csc⁡ycot⁡y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338265}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c064b7d-ae95-54c0-99a2-5b48ab4e6e2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3cot⁡y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336987} :answer-field/id #uuid "0033f394-fd38-57df-b724-8ce5cc5ff702" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338279}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2e4929ca-7804-5d00-ada3-f06fc525c774" :question/math-academy-id "q-71663" :question/problem "$\\int -3{csc}^{2}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y=$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\int {csc}^{2}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y=-cot⁡y+C\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int -3{csc}^{2}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y & =-3\\int {csc}^{2}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-3(-cot⁡y)+C \\\\  & =3cot⁡y+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338971 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064181 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9836abbe-e172-5c2f-8b19-145ef6d5c02d" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Cosecant Squared"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29f94094-a13e-59d3-8e01-6476ad259936" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡ytan⁡y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336421} {:answer/id #uuid "4bbf7e18-db54-5ad4-94b3-6740e294fa86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "tan⁡y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336736} {:answer/id #uuid "5a246749-d6a8-5b61-ab0c-eb37e92ba4e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-tan⁡y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336853} {:answer/id #uuid "7eef5879-dbe7-5b97-97a6-26e4a74a9d5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cot⁡y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337182} {:answer/id #uuid "cbbba5ec-56d3-5218-abf9-36a5e07211a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-cot⁡y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337821}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7eef5879-dbe7-5b97-97a6-26e4a74a9d5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cot⁡y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337182} :answer-field/id #uuid "709f437f-6931-5254-8a8a-e5df59c326ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338469}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e6d46060-4d10-5e18-98aa-e065865069a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-71664" :question/problem "$\\int -{csc}^{2}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y=$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\int {csc}^{2}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y=-cot⁡y+C\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int -{csc}^{2}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y & =-\\int {csc}^{2}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-(-cot⁡y)+C \\\\  & =cot⁡y+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338981 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059770 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "14d047c8-f032-5ff3-aced-65d40bfeeaad" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Augmented Matrix of a Linear System With Variables Other Than x, y, z"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ddc44f3-1a32-52a9-9031-b6a56a4c5108" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336161} {:answer/id #uuid "0ea49bc6-7ef7-57db-bb92-09c032a1d6f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336170} {:answer/id #uuid "30d03117-bdb7-5d97-9599-7a98774e36e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336498} {:answer/id #uuid "424af963-b37e-5232-9f21-c41fe8e366ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336659} {:answer/id #uuid "6e0b194c-4ec3-5eb9-8d66-12bf1b7385e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337008}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ddc44f3-1a32-52a9-9031-b6a56a4c5108" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336161} :answer-field/id #uuid "72df1cb2-a890-5c64-9b81-0a1f27f75747" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338472}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1731054d-ea13-5a85-8abe-142bb7f3ad7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-71734" :question/problem "What is the augmented matrix for the following system of linear equations?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}u-v=-1 \\\\ -u-v=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the system is already organized so that the variables are in the order $u,v$ on the left-hand side, the constants are on the right-hand side, and every variable has a coefficient.   \n\nThe coefficient matrix consists of the coefficients of the variables: \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe augmented matrix is the coefficient matrix augmented with the column that represents the right-hand sides of the equations:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064946 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064371 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "45b380ed-1974-5876-8c88-a45773741b48" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Speed of a Particle at a Particular Moment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "899c0898-cc24-5360-b626-1256d90ee17e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5cm/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063733} {:answer/id #uuid "c6aaecc1-1a6e-5ad3-9010-833d1cbbb472" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.0cm/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063734} {:answer/id #uuid "d2a83fe4-2550-5f4e-bded-f0e0495d3bad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5cm/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063735} {:answer/id #uuid "1db5a007-393b-5b2a-820b-7b0e9ee282a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.0cm/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063736} {:answer/id #uuid "3d517248-0d79-5d46-92c9-c67e3e69e7db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.0cm/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063737}] :answer-field/id #uuid "1533643d-1ad3-5fcd-8671-813502553b72" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063732} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "309fb741-f2ee-5d42-a410-6d3722af473d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5\\,\\text{cm/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393175} {:answer/id #uuid "0ba94734-6539-5f8b-b9fa-953023f4379c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5\\,\\text{cm/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393176} {:answer/id #uuid "827e042d-b06c-59c7-b91a-f5e840050abb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.0\\,\\text{cm/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393177} {:answer/id #uuid "dafb3fc0-f6a1-5617-964a-36080cd6f04c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.0\\,\\text{cm/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393178} {:answer/id #uuid "577baa3c-ff60-5e2e-9451-c051fd6e20b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.0\\,\\text{cm/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393179}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ba94734-6539-5f8b-b9fa-953023f4379c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5\\,\\text{cm/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393176} :answer-field/id #uuid "89bc4a65-3fa0-525a-8a5b-ebdeb649b1a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393174}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c20eb755-854c-51a7-aed2-16e438744a82" :question/math-academy-id "q-7175" :question/problem "The position $s(t)$ of a particle (in centimeters) at time $t$ seconds is given by \n\n$$\ns(t)=\\frac{5t-10}{t},\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the speed of the particle when $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{5t-10}{t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $t=2,$ the velocity is\n\n$$\nv(2)=\\frac{10}{(2)^{2}}=\\frac{10}{4}=2.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed is $|2.5|=2.5\\,\\text{cm/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338983 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064182 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90033e91-fdb2-5aa0-8cce-76762a38ffd2" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Using Arcsine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "753fa037-4869-57bb-ac4a-0f8226e93dce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337088} {:answer/id #uuid "7de44454-c056-5b46-a5ff-feadd65645cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337171} {:answer/id #uuid "af75991e-fe31-5097-bab1-f9f7833dfa8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337609} {:answer/id #uuid "cddee891-ba94-5bd0-9d64-b60f3812c423" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337835} {:answer/id #uuid "f64bcadb-6519-5fea-93f7-63aa02c92d1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338190}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7de44454-c056-5b46-a5ff-feadd65645cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337171} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6a689b3-7d92-5c9d-b9a2-6c4b6c753f86" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338710}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f5352827-e02f-55d1-bc3e-a103f93321ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-71761" :question/problem "$\\int (8x-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}})\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x+C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (8x-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}})\\text{d}x & =\\int 8x\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8\\int x\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338984 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064183 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7256d2e-7105-5a4e-8d0a-5e00dfc1e311" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Using Arctangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c05b9ce-0e54-531e-9602-9aa81d5949a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{5}+7\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336447} {:answer/id #uuid "627ef198-a214-5417-80e9-b45a792ad5f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{5}+7\\text{ arcsec }x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336916} {:answer/id #uuid "71c35c47-7b7b-5fef-b294-b9cb841b489c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{x}^{5}+7\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337048} {:answer/id #uuid "bb6f9d28-d3e5-53a1-a5e5-29186ee63437" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{5}+7\\text{ arccot }x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337694} {:answer/id #uuid "edeaae97-ac2c-562a-abdb-dd6daecb94ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{x}^{5}+7\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338111}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c05b9ce-0e54-531e-9602-9aa81d5949a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{5}+7\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336447} :answer-field/id #uuid "31ce09db-263d-5513-8b14-b7d2cd24d8f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338372}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8482c878-eb98-5b38-8c38-4c3a4a08ab5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-71768" :question/problem "If ${f}^{′}(x)=5{x}^{4}+\\frac{7}{1+{x}^{2}},$ then $f(x)=$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)=\\int {f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (5{x}^{4}+\\frac{7}{1+{x}^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 5{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{7}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\int {x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x+7\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}+7\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C \\\\  & ={x}^{5}+7\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393180 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087889 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0cdcbc5-c10b-5dd1-af9a-410d2d44a4c5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Acceleration of a Particle Given Its Velocity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89cf84e6-fa60-5c64-b93b-865747b163cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(t)=1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393182} {:answer/id #uuid "ce91f5ac-68b0-5d12-b408-5805719d5633" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(t)=1-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393183} {:answer/id #uuid "9153eb0d-2401-550a-8773-be6e4eb8e8c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(t)=\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393184} {:answer/id #uuid "adae588b-ff36-562f-b371-73f948870d37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(t)=t+\\frac{1}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393185} {:answer/id #uuid "d5256482-56c1-5d4e-a0c1-dc867d2591d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(t)=t-\\frac{1}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393186}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89cf84e6-fa60-5c64-b93b-865747b163cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(t)=1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393182} :answer-field/id #uuid "0524bc50-6e64-5c83-9e81-cb4394c0bfc4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393181}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2e5248e4-d160-51d4-9bba-1d38c3c5aabb" :question/math-academy-id "q-7186" :question/problem "The velocity $v$ of a particle at time $t$ is given by $v(t)=\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{t}.$ Calculate the acceleration of the particle at time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate $v(t)$ to calculate $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-\\frac{1}{t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-{t}^{-1}) \\\\  & =1-(-{t}^{-2}) \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339007 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087700 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a75c67e5-3469-5730-9181-54df4ec6d5b2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Inverse of a Larger Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43339e1d-1bfa-55d0-b3c2-c8e4ad7e48be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336667} {:answer/id #uuid "7483dbfb-2a05-5002-b015-71ac4bbb50b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337079} {:answer/id #uuid "9bc56d9f-7969-5603-8164-402febf66e59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337442} {:answer/id #uuid "b2a235c2-3d22-5e37-8f24-ef0a23a374c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337626} {:answer/id #uuid "cf0d9494-e399-55f1-8b32-0a285f986f3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337843}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2a235c2-3d22-5e37-8f24-ef0a23a374c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337626} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b488541-8354-522a-b07d-347ec327f286" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338298}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8aec4565-777c-5fd0-95ff-9f5ced653b55" :question/math-academy-id "q-71879" :question/problem "Consider the matrices\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & -1 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $AB=BA={I}_{3}$\n\nII. $BC=CB={I}_{3}$\n\nIII. $A={B}^{-1}$" :question/worked-solution "- First, we check whether $AB=BA={I}_{3}.$ Computing $AB$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}∗ & -1 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne {I}_{3}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is false. As a result, $A\\ne {B}^{-1}$ and statement III is false too.\n\n- Now, we check whether $BC=CB={I}_{3}.$ Computing $BC$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & -1 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{3}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, computing $CB$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CB & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & -1 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{3}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403502 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176099 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bee6d4bf-79da-5eb3-a214-8c045dbce5bd" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity of a Particle at the Moment When Its Acceleration is Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e800b9e5-7a04-5a52-83f0-e3afcf5ec850" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=4,\\,v=10.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403504} {:answer/id #uuid "353897c1-d771-528a-9201-b28cc593f791" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=4.5,\\,v=27.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403505} {:answer/id #uuid "351b1c52-f2f1-52fc-bd66-0b6482b93763" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=4.5,\\,v=12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403506} {:answer/id #uuid "e50e1c45-ecbd-5feb-9363-3cbef9942625" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=3.5,\\,v=26.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403507} {:answer/id #uuid "61d395f2-5e29-597d-bed3-c8a2e676c54f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=4,\\,v=22.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403508}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "353897c1-d771-528a-9201-b28cc593f791" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=4.5,\\,v=27.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403505} :answer-field/id #uuid "f245a54a-e7b7-5958-90da-5850b106775b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403503}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e823f661-78ed-5c49-931f-9f7b092bd2fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-7188" :question/problem "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t)=-\\frac{2}{9}{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}+14t+17,\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{2}{9}{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}+14t+17) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{2}+6t+14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{2}+6t+14) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}t+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =-\\frac{4}{3}t+6 \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}t & =6 \\\\ t & =4.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the acceleration is zero when $t=4.5.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\nv(4.5)=-\\frac{2}{3}(4.5)^{2}+6(4.5)+14=27.5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339009 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064171 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f9548e6-28ef-5a97-ab04-dfb1b6e23c16" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating the Exponential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23784fc4-3a90-53e4-89bb-df7f53cf6d59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336365} {:answer/id #uuid "2c3c2a30-99f8-537b-8508-e5713aa27c9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336450} {:answer/id #uuid "67383a40-44c5-5a00-bfa0-07f13c0dcbe6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336953} {:answer/id #uuid "b945ae93-7849-5d6e-b645-1e8c14a1d6cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{1/2x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337675} {:answer/id #uuid "fbb744da-ccfd-5f44-8169-07909ef53f32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338242}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23784fc4-3a90-53e4-89bb-df7f53cf6d59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336365} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9fc2e0f-aa1f-50b5-8ba0-74df5403bd7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338718}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7119fae9-f265-530e-8dcf-9a8fd6ab825e" :question/math-academy-id "q-71900" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Factoring out the constant $\\frac{1}{2}$ and calculating the resulting integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339012 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092386 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "44f09c22-7107-5c80-9c91-a7b58cb639b2" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative From a Table Using a Forward Difference Approximation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cd0adb6-1bd2-5705-9d99-f43d55b4d0bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336152} {:answer/id #uuid "12826623-37f5-5d60-82a4-81c8304ec69f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336208} {:answer/id #uuid "23db118c-27be-5c6d-b298-fab8ffd8ebb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336369} {:answer/id #uuid "983e0a91-3664-58ec-a60b-1abfffdd593f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337420} {:answer/id #uuid "fc8da3a7-b71b-51b9-848c-28049a316986" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338253}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12826623-37f5-5d60-82a4-81c8304ec69f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336208} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3604013-2c08-5a12-919c-0ddaa43dfc91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338580}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0a435c9a-da6e-5d88-b3b2-e3df2e3a1da1" :question/math-academy-id "q-71915" :question/problem "A function $p(t)$ has values according to the table below:\n\n| $t$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $p(t)$ | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ | $5$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nApproximate ${p}^{′}(t)$ at the point $t=0$ using a forward difference approximation." :question/worked-solution "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\n{p}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{p(t+h)-p(t)}{h}\n$$\n\nat the point $t=0.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=1.$ This gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{′}(0) & ≈\\frac{p(0+1)-p(0)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{p(1)-p(0)}{1} \\\\  & =5-9 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339014 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092387 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "419134c3-3177-5d7d-86f6-899a73338b1c" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative From a Graph Using a Forward Difference Approximation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a2f0ad6-e62c-58a2-9002-9bab955f7c3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336425} {:answer/id #uuid "6ca5a669-e3b2-532b-a3fd-de673bfe5433" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336996} {:answer/id #uuid "b8f5dc7a-4b60-596c-a77c-4c1880566b85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337671} {:answer/id #uuid "cb2ae886-e224-5d41-9e6a-e9cd18e64fea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337817} {:answer/id #uuid "e523cecf-ac3c-528d-b904-4c25cb796b39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338029}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb2ae886-e224-5d41-9e6a-e9cd18e64fea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337817} :answer-field/id #uuid "960ebabc-88cc-5fb7-a072-2b46fe3d5a3d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338555}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a7cfbba0-e1ec-5e2b-8311-cfe63691a6aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-71921" :question/problem "![](images/21/21e310adffbad6309176ea26549e0e43efe3d8e3d7f2f0b018902be16781802d.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=g(x)$ is shown above. Approximate ${g}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=1.5$ using a forward difference approximation with step size $h=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{g(x+h)-g(x)}{h}\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=1.5.$ Applying the above formula with $x=1.5$ and $h=1$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(1.5) & ≈\\frac{g(1.5+1)-g(1.5)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{g(2.5)-g(1.5)}{1} \\\\  & =4-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339023 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096843 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0a312840-0d35-50bb-94b3-b04778f4efc3" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Function Using the Chain Rule and Prime Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d2f6517-e258-513a-b912-2250e7c54e17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15(2+5x{)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336461} {:answer/id #uuid "3b2c8729-231c-5b49-a873-246a32aa4645" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15(2-5x{)}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336588} {:answer/id #uuid "77d335ef-89db-5392-8e96-2e5663d37f72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15(2+5x{)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337111} {:answer/id #uuid "84c34e15-f7f7-51bd-91ad-be1849bcac42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15(2-5x{)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337229} {:answer/id #uuid "ba6755f1-59bc-5601-b289-66317d2b0d63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15(2-5x{)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337682}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba6755f1-59bc-5601-b289-66317d2b0d63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15(2-5x{)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337682} :answer-field/id #uuid "20dca297-5492-547a-957c-959f9e7520f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338339}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ccad926b-6344-5bec-99bc-507d50cda1e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-71956" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={(2-5x)}^{3},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=2-5x$ and $f(g(x))=g(x{)}^{3}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{3}{)}^{′}=3{g}^{2}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(2-5x{)}^{′}=-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =3{g}^{2}\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =-15(2-5x{)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339025 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087765 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c514865c-02d0-5d59-938b-fdd739cdb257" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Velocity Vector Given the Acceleration Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02b1e407-9f34-5917-a8e8-6429929466cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=⟨t+4,2t+3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336056} {:answer/id #uuid "14e6a034-a578-53fc-b1ce-917071816975" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=⟨3t+1,4t+2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336230} {:answer/id #uuid "d3f11555-6307-5255-ae09-7413e8abe54d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=⟨4t+3,3t+1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337885} {:answer/id #uuid "e8f4a806-a467-5bec-b324-ee521855fcf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=⟨2t+4,4t+2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338065} {:answer/id #uuid "feba51d7-bede-5389-9736-715ce5cf40e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=⟨t+3,2t+1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338269}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "feba51d7-bede-5389-9736-715ce5cf40e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=⟨t+3,2t+1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338269} :answer-field/id #uuid "c96e533a-33a6-500d-a486-1991eb428088" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338638}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "80cdc2c0-175f-5f4a-ab26-00baffb7a933" :question/math-academy-id "q-7196" :question/problem "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na=⟨1,2⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨4,3⟩$ when $t=1$, calculate the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨1,2⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 1\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 2\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t+{C}_{1},2t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(1)=⟨4,3⟩.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨1+{C}_{1},2(1)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨4,3⟩ & =⟨1+{C}_{1},2+{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =1+{C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{1} & =3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =2+{C}_{2} \\\\ {C}_{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\nv=⟨t+3,2t+1⟩.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339030 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087703 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e64e0c3-f8f5-5c38-ae75-1aed92e0ac01" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Variable in a Singular Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f062ffd-0731-5c9d-b5df-1e280483ad62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336623} {:answer/id #uuid "87e8b87b-9219-5612-bc47-0727af4eb0b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337259} {:answer/id #uuid "9d1a9b12-eb65-52f2-be17-471977096571" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337454} {:answer/id #uuid "db1521d6-6365-5c27-8635-662375c34db6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337942} {:answer/id #uuid "e0112779-824d-50fa-a23d-b42b80a0e397" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337989}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87e8b87b-9219-5612-bc47-0727af4eb0b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337259} :answer-field/id #uuid "82b667e3-83f0-533b-abd1-94355475f197" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338510}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "35c7dc25-96f0-5d9f-9d6a-dfbf5fcfb320" :question/math-academy-id "q-71969" :question/problem "If $M=[\\begin{aligned}t & 4 \\\\ t & 10\\end{aligned}]$ is a singular matrix, calculate the value of $t.$" :question/worked-solution "The matrix $M$ is singular if and only if $det(M)=0.$ Calculating the determinant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =t\\cdot 10-t\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =10t-4t \\\\  & =6t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the determinant equal to $0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6t & =0 \\\\ t & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $t$ is $t=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373160 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087734 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9be02ade-3dd2-5ec3-a532-78d1151dc89f" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Complex Number Shown on an Argand Diagram in Exponential Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92a3192e-9980-5b6e-995e-e901475c202b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=4\\sqrt{2}\\,{\\text{e}}^{5\\pi \\text{i}/4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373162} {:answer/id #uuid "8e7a03da-376f-5f43-b2e3-5bb08e406633" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=3\\sqrt{2}\\,{\\text{e}}^{5\\pi \\text{i}/4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373163} {:answer/id #uuid "aeff9ff1-81f5-544c-a33e-0126a1e4496d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=4\\,{\\text{e}}^{3\\pi \\text{i}/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373164} {:answer/id #uuid "a70e13e0-8903-5218-89fa-1105c69fb4f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=4\\,{\\text{e}}^{3\\pi \\text{i}/4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373165} {:answer/id #uuid "4b187e20-0d76-5b76-bb07-602904c533d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=4\\sqrt{2}\\,{\\text{e}}^{3\\pi \\text{i}/4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373166}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92a3192e-9980-5b6e-995e-e901475c202b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=4\\sqrt{2}\\,{\\text{e}}^{5\\pi \\text{i}/4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373162} :answer-field/id #uuid "22bd1b3e-2ba7-599f-8f8a-d8820b27736b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373161}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2261451e-0663-5bd3-96e6-ee90552101b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-71974" :question/problem "![](images/18/18f848a65a00388e813290220a6b039652728d703ae7c07d34a755fc0c82f05c.png)\n\nWrite the complex number $z$, shown on the Argand diagram above, in exponential form." :question/worked-solution "The complex number shown in the Argand diagram can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x+\\text{i}y \\\\  & =-4-4\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo write it in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $z$ lies in the third quadrant, we can find the angle $\\theta $ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\pi +|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =\\pi +|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{-4}{-4})| \\\\  & =\\pi +|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)| \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=4\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get \n\n$$\nz=4\\sqrt{2}\\,{\\text{e}}^{5\\pi \\text{i}/4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339036 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087735 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01caf021-de3e-5e2a-89ce-ba289b65c0ff" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Complex Number in Exponential Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19ba13a7-a8af-592d-b200-b237e09d7313" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=3\\,{\\text{e}}^{3\\pi \\text{i}/4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336269} {:answer/id #uuid "3f18755a-6590-52bf-825a-3181c701a71d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=2\\,{\\text{e}}^{5\\pi \\text{i}/6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336625} {:answer/id #uuid "74eb7548-5b21-5ab1-b315-9aba5080f6e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=4\\,{\\text{e}}^{2\\pi \\text{i}/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337086} {:answer/id #uuid "a7fb877f-dfa1-5ad1-a96d-bc31e563bd26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=4\\,{\\text{e}}^{5\\pi \\text{i}/6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337551} {:answer/id #uuid "ce965180-c460-5735-ba71-a16c78ddb8a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=2\\,{\\text{e}}^{2\\pi \\text{i}/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337839}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74eb7548-5b21-5ab1-b315-9aba5080f6e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=4\\,{\\text{e}}^{2\\pi \\text{i}/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337086} :answer-field/id #uuid "2137d245-5e38-588c-bf3d-27b3cfc9ed11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338340}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a3bc38ff-6706-540c-bfc8-3a2f9e0a0e13" :question/math-academy-id "q-71982" :question/problem "Write the complex number $z=-2+2\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}$ in exponential form." :question/worked-solution "To write our complex number in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{(2\\sqrt{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $z$ lies in the second quadrant, we can find the angle $\\theta $ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{2})| \\\\  & =\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})| \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=4$ and $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get \n\n$$\nz=4\\,{\\text{e}}^{2\\pi \\text{i}/3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339038 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087846 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "37a4168b-1425-5f3a-a577-4b5884906a52" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Sum of a Complex Number and Its Conjugate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "131e439a-9165-56c6-8fdd-b21c8dea91e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8+14\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336212} {:answer/id #uuid "1b6aa3b5-1f23-581f-bd0e-52f0682b50a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336285} {:answer/id #uuid "1e6f5eb4-3b18-507b-9c32-4ce046ab8046" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336313} {:answer/id #uuid "d95dea67-8da9-5f2b-8c58-9a5fb247332b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337923} {:answer/id #uuid "f90cfae7-9eea-5dc8-8b18-628804095bea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338215}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b6aa3b5-1f23-581f-bd0e-52f0682b50a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336285} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd3178b7-71a4-5c4f-9b23-601af5c49da4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338617}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "568e1d1c-4c3e-536f-acb0-c6800da06527" :question/math-academy-id "q-71986" :question/problem "If $z=4+7\\text{i}$, calculate $z+\\overset{―}{z}.$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nWhen we add a complex number and its conjugate, the result is always twice the real part of the complex number.\n\nFor the given complex number $z=4+7\\text{i},$ the real part is $Re(z)=4,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+\\overset{―}{z} & =2\\text{Re}(z) \\\\  & =2(4) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nAlternatively, we can actually carry out the process of adding the complex number and its conjugate.\n\nTo obtain the conjugate of the complex number $z=4+7\\text{i},$ we flip the sign of the imaginary part and get $\\overset{―}{z}=4-7\\text{i}.$\n\nAdding, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+\\overset{―}{z} & =(4+7\\text{i})+(4-7\\text{i}) \\\\  & =(4+4)+\\text{i}(7-7) \\\\  & =8+\\text{i}0 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064947 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064272 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa38f112-466c-50e9-ac08-be72b6f53782" :knowledge-point/title "Relating a Derivative to a Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "148eb00f-3a9c-5cde-b11e-4aa6e5df5baf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N(t)=r(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336227} {:answer/id #uuid "3ce9ccdf-1421-5fcb-b41e-795e91107452" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N(t)={r}^{′}(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336606} {:answer/id #uuid "3d4ac506-d0f2-599b-b8f3-e6dce6983161" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{N}^{′}(t)=r(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336611} {:answer/id #uuid "80a522d2-bd93-5515-9735-bcaf1eb17692" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N(t)=-{r}^{′}(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337197} {:answer/id #uuid "e3743113-4ad5-561d-af41-cbb9230afbba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{N}^{′}(t)=-r(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338017}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d4ac506-d0f2-599b-b8f3-e6dce6983161" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{N}^{′}(t)=r(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336611} :answer-field/id #uuid "874c5434-9157-538e-be59-176244d35d61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338525}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "97f8f938-0d50-59f5-922b-f7bea8bb44f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-72004" :question/problem "Jason, a biologist, is measuring the growth of bacteria in a Petri dish by recording the following two numbers for different values of $t,$ where $t$ is the time, in hours, since he started his observations:\n\n- $N(t)$ - the number of bacteria in the dish.\n\n- $r(t)$ - the rate at which the number of bacteria in the dish is increasing.\n\nWhat is the relationship between $N(t)$ and $r(t)?$" :question/worked-solution "The function $N(t)$ gives the number of bacteria inside the dish after $t$ hours. Then,\n\n$$\n{N}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}N(t)}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{Change in }N}{\\text{Change in }t}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${N}^{′}(t)$ gives the rate at which the number of bacteria is increasing at time $t$, which is the same as $r(t).$ Therefore, the relationship between $N(t)$ and $r(t)$ is ${N}^{′}(t)=r(t).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339052 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105715 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf98ee24-7774-5674-8a1e-63f4f6d225c0" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Reciprocal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f88c79e-e25c-5fcd-a473-3f9adb3d4e9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{7}ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336327} {:answer/id #uuid "58b493c2-73d3-5db1-af15-0200a2937632" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{ln⁡|x|}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336844} {:answer/id #uuid "daf20704-2d65-5753-af90-f6e026c52e12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{7}ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337940} {:answer/id #uuid "ec4261ce-b5b3-5551-9fbd-56937e1eea24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{\\sqrt{7}}ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338091} {:answer/id #uuid "f7a97ce9-ff10-5758-b496-1ffe87d0bb3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-ln⁡|\\sqrt{7}x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338202}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f88c79e-e25c-5fcd-a473-3f9adb3d4e9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{7}ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336327} :answer-field/id #uuid "f930863c-55bb-55cc-bd9d-51b8ae4abea3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338715}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d9daecf1-e255-5c0c-a433-fdc798f551aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-72059" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{7}}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{7}$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\frac{1}{x},$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{7}ln⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339055 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092242 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b175cb1-6615-540f-9145-224a838e37d0" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Graph Using a Backward Difference Approximation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fe41587-c3f7-5479-b239-47cfa8fef036" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336633} {:answer/id #uuid "5d43e33c-77e5-5307-a409-7bca8540a7e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336877} {:answer/id #uuid "809e0d21-914e-52aa-99d0-f75bf9d82085" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337196} {:answer/id #uuid "b32bc383-231f-5724-a292-f0e334d095ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337630} {:answer/id #uuid "cc219e1b-1b2d-5eef-92e1-8216c629b871" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337823}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d43e33c-77e5-5307-a409-7bca8540a7e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336877} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d180c05-ad48-5e0d-92e1-00bb1d157e3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338566}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c7480396-2066-56b4-9d58-774c761ad733" :question/math-academy-id "q-72074" :question/problem "![](images/89/894d659f3ef4544eeda653f5e098d9ca26f3e06899798441534a1bf395628d0e.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $f(x)$ is shown above. \nApproximate ${f}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=3$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h=2.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{f(x)-f(x-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=3.$ Applying the above formula with $x=3$ and $h=2$ gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(3) & ≈\\frac{f(3)-f(3-2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(3)-f(1)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-(-1)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129850 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129173 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8537cc22-8d18-57c8-9ebf-921bd4d06ae7" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Double Summation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ee3daa0-63ea-57fd-8203-29e8ffd77480" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336174} {:answer/id #uuid "12b2522f-4758-5d90-8080-b542234b7547" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336210} {:answer/id #uuid "39fbd0e6-1e9a-5d05-a795-60d8cfddca47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336577} {:answer/id #uuid "d237224f-7536-5f2e-8a8f-f0e65701610b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337874} {:answer/id #uuid "f10ed6bc-4030-5ec3-a3e8-ed644aee61ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338143}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39fbd0e6-1e9a-5d05-a795-60d8cfddca47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336577} :answer-field/id #uuid "58439c8e-2c44-5252-9a32-9e790d2edd09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338427}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6e16a192-c647-58f3-9bd2-bc5597aebc2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-72094" :question/problem "${\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}ij=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write out the inner summation by summing over the index $i:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}ij & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}[{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}ij] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}(1j+2j+3j) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}(j+2j+3j) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}6j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we sum over the index $j:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}6j & =6\\cdot 1+6\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6+12 \\\\  & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339059 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064152 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "159b4d58-b34d-52cd-aad2-2c058f772ad2" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Derivative of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08f73173-53b3-594c-9f00-73f6211f3aba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The rate at which the perimeter changes as each side changes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336106} {:answer/id #uuid "2f86ab33-c24f-513c-9f78-18726fd0321b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The rate at which the area changes as each side changes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336488} {:answer/id #uuid "8526b1a3-6e9c-580f-9c0b-82c5736c43b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The rate at which each side changes as the perimeter changes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337231} {:answer/id #uuid "95ba1001-8463-59e2-bed5-38495c9afaab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The rate at which the perimeter changes as the area changes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337400} {:answer/id #uuid "eddb83ed-12b2-59b9-8dd1-0e4ed245941a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The rate at which each side changes as the area changes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338109}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08f73173-53b3-594c-9f00-73f6211f3aba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The rate at which the perimeter changes as each side changes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336106} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa385052-bba0-5f25-abff-7baf2aaa746f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338590}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c47d8541-4bf8-5eff-901b-cba03fe2cd37" :question/math-academy-id "q-72104" :question/problem "Consider a regular pentagon such that the length $s$ of each side varies. What is the best interpretation of ${P}^{′}(s)$ if $P$ is the perimeter of the pentagon?" :question/worked-solution "The function $P(s)$ gives the perimeter of a pentagon with side $s.$ So,\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(s)=\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}s}=\\frac{\\text{Change in }P}{\\text{Change in }s}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${P}^{′}(s)$ is the rate at which the perimeter changes as each side changes."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339060 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064272 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa38f112-466c-50e9-ac08-be72b6f53782" :knowledge-point/title "Relating a Derivative to a Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bd3314e-09b5-5ed6-b34d-9c8eb2cd078f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{A}^{′}(t)=r(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336291} {:answer/id #uuid "5f89b95a-dc2f-5355-a891-5f7ad0897f2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A(t)=-{r}^{′}(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336898} {:answer/id #uuid "d84adbce-e1e5-541f-ae42-248e49f85aef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{A}^{′}(t)=-r(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337909} {:answer/id #uuid "eb90804a-9cb1-5c0e-93ff-93893405ab87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A(t)={r}^{′}(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338088} {:answer/id #uuid "f843d514-c114-567f-8b4e-d0d7f91897e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A(t)=r(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338207}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1bd3314e-09b5-5ed6-b34d-9c8eb2cd078f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{A}^{′}(t)=r(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336291} :answer-field/id #uuid "27c52aef-834f-5c1c-9709-b11a313c17d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338353}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4da77317-b047-59f4-8384-7a00ca57d0df" :question/math-academy-id "q-72105" :question/problem "Douglas, a pilot, is flying a plane. The plane's control systems record two measurements for different values of $t,$ where $t$ is the time, in minutes, since take off. The two measurements are:\n\n- $A(t)$ - the altitude (height) of the plane\n\n- $r(t)$ the rate at which the altitude is increasing\n\nWhat is the relationship between $A(t)$ and $r(t)?$" :question/worked-solution "The function $A(t)$ gives the altitude of the plane after $t$ hours. Then,\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}A(t)}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{Change in }A}{\\text{Change in }t}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${A}^{′}(t)$ gives the rate at which the altitude is increasing at time $t$, which is the same as $r(t).$ Therefore, the relationship between $A(t)$ and $r(t)$ is ${A}^{′}(t)=r(t).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339064 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064283 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ec11481-cea2-514b-aa78-707fcf8838cc" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Power of a Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b52aa7c5-0426-5772-8576-0d06507ee246" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-28\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{sin}^{6}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337642} {:answer/id #uuid "bca0ac11-5889-590d-b18f-dc821e5d6c5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-28{cos}^{6}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337708} {:answer/id #uuid "ec2962a1-647e-5d8b-9492-8e3b141a6e17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-28{sin}^{6}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338090} {:answer/id #uuid "f9f6bad8-7c0c-55c3-aec1-1f849a332783" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{sin}^{6}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338224} {:answer/id #uuid "ff8e2ee2-8bc8-5f48-a816-f19ea88144d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{cos}^{6}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338274}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bca0ac11-5889-590d-b18f-dc821e5d6c5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-28{cos}^{6}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337708} :answer-field/id #uuid "05250791-7660-5c5c-b045-5bac1563c939" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338283}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6e3f0d37-02ff-5992-af83-461909d51264" :question/math-academy-id "q-72157" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ when $f(x)=4{cos}^{7}⁡(x).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express\n\n$$\nf(x)=4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{7}.\n$$\n\nNow, we see that $f(x)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=4{u}^{7},\\,u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=28{u}^{6},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =28{u}^{6}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =28(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{6}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-28{cos}^{6}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403537 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078483 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "47989094-35d2-561f-add4-f1d427cd8a20" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Position Vector Given a Velocity Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "783ded5c-224b-59ae-b32b-ec19000aa60d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}+2,\\,2{t}^{4}-42⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403539} {:answer/id #uuid "06d02cfb-4919-5c32-955c-bd2e1e9b658c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨{t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}+2,\\,{t}^{4}-24⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403540} {:answer/id #uuid "ad5eefc7-8bc8-5e3a-b849-484cdc6a8daa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}+2,\\,2{t}^{4}-24⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403541} {:answer/id #uuid "f970c01f-ad2a-5aad-bf67-ace81ba81fd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨{t}^{3}-{t}^{2},\\,2{t}^{4}-42⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403542} {:answer/id #uuid "dd440fad-abbb-55d5-b8ef-41b50f1b49d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}+12,\\,2{t}^{4}-72⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403543}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd440fad-abbb-55d5-b8ef-41b50f1b49d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}+12,\\,2{t}^{4}-72⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403543} :answer-field/id #uuid "a072aa51-1711-5bcb-b3f7-621b3a6f46ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403538}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4e1070d0-6a3b-5c88-bdfe-23bfbc7b2d02" :question/math-academy-id "q-7217" :question/problem "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv=⟨t(3t-2),\\,8{t}^{3}⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $(30,90)$ when $t=3,$ calculate the position vector of $P$ at time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨t(3t-2),\\,8{t}^{3}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int t(3t-2)\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 8{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}+{C}_{1},\\,2{t}^{4}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(3)=⟨30,90⟩.$ Substituting $t=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(3) & =⟨(3)^{3}-(3)^{2}+{C}_{1},\\,2(3)^{4}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨30,90⟩ & =⟨18+{C}_{1},162+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=12$ and ${C}_{2}=-72.$ Therefore, the position vector is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}+12,\\,2{t}^{4}-72⟩.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339066 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064284 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a483e892-90f2-51f4-80cd-2b1200cd0c37" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Composition of Trigonometric Functions Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35d0b5a0-960c-537b-b36b-f81b09719172" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336532} {:answer/id #uuid "8659cf38-7f28-521b-9eb6-a1f4bb5d55a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337248} {:answer/id #uuid "cf2b4496-4d43-50c5-b8f4-50cbe3748941" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337845} {:answer/id #uuid "e5a21dd0-44ef-54d6-bc1c-1c98a20cbcd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338037} {:answer/id #uuid "f7b439b8-4fb4-5bd2-b82d-c5283f54f75b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7b439b8-4fb4-5bd2-b82d-c5283f54f75b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338204} :answer-field/id #uuid "76164faf-c99c-514d-8f55-17d4f55b16c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338480}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bdadc1fd-0dca-59ce-bd49-88a477cdb0a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-72173" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $y=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡u,\\,u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡u\\operatorname{tan}⁡u,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sec}⁡u\\operatorname{tan}⁡u\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079196 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078504 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "279c5e4e-db19-584b-becb-10864fcba188" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Position Vector Given an Acceleration Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cab57635-f418-5983-befd-a20ffd9eccbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r = ⟨t^{3} - t^{2}, 2t^{2} - 2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077955} {:answer/id #uuid "07395ddc-381a-5351-aed1-72fc51cba8f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r = ⟨t^{3} - t, 2t^{2} - 2t + 6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077956} {:answer/id #uuid "07bdef2f-8f89-5da7-a451-659a422289ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r = ⟨2t^{3} - 2t^{2}, t^{2} - t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077957} {:answer/id #uuid "5813ad31-b965-5f02-8647-c303e979a41a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r = ⟨t^{3} - 2t^{2} + 4t, t^{2} - 2t + 6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077958} {:answer/id #uuid "6a559519-ac1d-518c-aa1f-2d4402a2ad96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r = ⟨t^{3} + 4t, t^{2} - t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077959}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ce4a844e-d0ea-58aa-9301-81a7518883c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077954} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fb179c5-0c95-5eb7-b275-9ca460f5d11c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨{t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}+4t,\\,{t}^{2}-2t+6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403545} {:answer/id #uuid "cbbdc519-f4c7-5a08-a7dd-3f88bedcf9c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨2{t}^{3}-2{t}^{2},\\,{t}^{2}-t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403546} {:answer/id #uuid "3a4412af-fcc5-5a70-8cc1-058a234b657a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨{t}^{3}-{t}^{2},\\,2{t}^{2}-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403547} {:answer/id #uuid "4f5fe75f-182c-5dc8-bb6d-6b2a54417938" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨{t}^{3}-t,\\,2{t}^{2}-2t+6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403548} {:answer/id #uuid "4892e60d-0f8b-54a6-a9fb-aedd25ed041e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨{t}^{3}+4t,\\,{t}^{2}-t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403549}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a4412af-fcc5-5a70-8cc1-058a234b657a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨{t}^{3}-{t}^{2},\\,2{t}^{2}-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403547} :answer-field/id #uuid "66b0a37a-393b-5f66-b851-8ee2229ca9f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403544}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "83a0e0a1-0c0d-5f48-8e86-38cbeffbfb71" :question/math-academy-id "q-7221" :question/problem "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨6t-2,4⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $(0,0)$ and has velocity $⟨1,\\,4⟩$ when $t=1,$ calculate the position vector of $P$ at time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector. To find the velocity vector, we first need to integrate the acceleration vector. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨6t-2,4⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int (6t-2)\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 4\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{t}^{2}-2t+{C}_{1},\\,4t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(1)=⟨1,4⟩.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨3(1)^{2}-2(1)+{C}_{1},\\,4(1)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,4⟩ & =⟨1+{C}_{1},4+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨3{t}^{2}-2t,\\,4t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the position vector by integrating the velocity vector.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨3{t}^{2}-2t,\\,4t⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int (3{t}^{2}-2t)\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 4t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}+{C}_{3},\\,2{t}^{2}+{C}_{4}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(1)=⟨0,0⟩.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨(1)^{3}-(1)^{2}+{C}_{3},\\,2(1)^{2}+{C}_{4}⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,0⟩ & =⟨{C}_{3},2+{C}_{4}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{3}=0$ and ${C}_{4}=-2.$ Therefore, the position of $P$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨{t}^{3}-{t}^{2},\\,2{t}^{2}-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339074 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054677 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "619182bf-107d-5217-a306-ec4f7c1109eb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Function Given in Implicit Form Using the Product Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a0fc10a-2cbd-5633-bb2f-ea7aaaa841c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)}{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)+3{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336123} {:answer/id #uuid "16292cbd-8350-5bd3-96d9-795ef552cf0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)}{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)+3{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336240} {:answer/id #uuid "1e57b365-540c-5e8a-8331-f6790e70b06d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)}{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)+2{y}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336311} {:answer/id #uuid "23a544b4-0d38-57bb-983c-e904eb2a25c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)}{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336367} {:answer/id #uuid "ee47ac7c-c346-5761-8902-7e42ed6ca55e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)}{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)+3{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338116}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16292cbd-8350-5bd3-96d9-795ef552cf0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)}{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)+3{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336240} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6bf4581-0ffc-537f-82d9-87405f04b109" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338711}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "454b7cf5-7862-57c7-a910-8dbc0dac1fcc" :question/math-academy-id "q-72213" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)+{y}^{3}=2{x}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)+{y}^{3}=2{x}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)+{y}^{3}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y))+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{3}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}) \\\\ [x(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)]+3{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6{x}^{2} \\\\ x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)+3{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)+3{y}^{2}) & =6{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{6{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)}{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)+3{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393201 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096810 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b47a8b7-722d-564e-8e7a-ad07577d6f6b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Rate of Change of an XY-Coordinate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21689172-4d3c-58f4-ab05-b3ede303077e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393203} {:answer/id #uuid "f697959f-44b8-5210-9f7e-1979d2a5ae7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393204} {:answer/id #uuid "586238f7-2f99-5a8a-9664-756d8fb42861" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393205} {:answer/id #uuid "21422ed5-2d8c-51a5-ac65-50e7a6c83355" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393206} {:answer/id #uuid "454121d4-7104-549f-b640-3e2b0c443a68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393207}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "454121d4-7104-549f-b640-3e2b0c443a68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393207} :answer-field/id #uuid "db06fc53-130e-5887-91ac-6c046aa2906d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393202}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a4c40fff-4d18-5542-8848-571c276df41d" :question/math-academy-id "q-72231" :question/problem "A particle $P$ is moving along the curve $y={x}^{2}-6x$ such that the $x$-coordinate of $P$ is changing at the rate of $3\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.$ Find the rate of change of the $y$-coordinate when $x=2\\,\\text{cm}.$" :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={x}^{2}-6x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}-6x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-6x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2x-6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=3\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}$ and $x=2$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =3\\cdot (2\\cdot 2-6)\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s} \\\\  & =-6\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373181 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087833 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "57e19ee3-db9a-58d7-8ca3-4e8801aee656" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Tall Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6805f839-3bcd-57e0-afc7-fccf7bbdeae6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{25}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373183} {:answer/id #uuid "2365b4a0-4a4e-5c09-8c6f-06c3940e1f92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{25}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373184} {:answer/id #uuid "1389bd33-3c64-5352-8c10-4e2a1ccf402e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{10}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373185} {:answer/id #uuid "50ed5beb-40df-5cbb-b518-b7088703743f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{10}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373186} {:answer/id #uuid "a7318fc4-07ec-5dda-9f48-5c543101ca23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373187}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2365b4a0-4a4e-5c09-8c6f-06c3940e1f92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{25}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373184} :answer-field/id #uuid "630a029c-6e06-5fd5-84a7-4a8213f014cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373182}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f15691a7-e2ba-5a41-93ce-4813f75267cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-72251" :question/problem "![](images/82/8289d16711c1ce0a97e056b44acd949908ed97ce6ae2e4c68d1e5b9022846d01.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(-3,2),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=2,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=5.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-(-3))^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{25} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339078 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087833 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "57e19ee3-db9a-58d7-8ca3-4e8801aee656" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Tall Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d360012-b71b-5e3d-a5ed-050551fedc08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{6}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{8}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336156} {:answer/id #uuid "363d6738-eccc-55d0-b37d-dc6eeb5492c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{16}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336536} {:answer/id #uuid "799fad89-4775-5c00-9f34-a99bb77a3ae6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{6}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337125} {:answer/id #uuid "975b13c5-78a1-50ac-8fca-df15265468b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{16}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337411} {:answer/id #uuid "a0e5af31-ccd8-5042-a136-b3b71e9698e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{16}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337486}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "975b13c5-78a1-50ac-8fca-df15265468b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{16}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337411} :answer-field/id #uuid "e195d8c3-ee86-56e6-9272-e56918978464" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338674}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "82c10773-d500-51bb-9b6a-76b40d3c3844" :question/math-academy-id "q-72252" :question/problem "![](images/28/28079ead5ebb4649900653172d11c25f3ff489c6b34772094a1ba947c18ef3e1.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(-2,-2),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=3,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=4.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-(-2))^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-(-2))^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{16} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339079 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054677 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "619182bf-107d-5217-a306-ec4f7c1109eb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Function Given in Implicit Form Using the Product Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b322d9c-37ab-52f7-9c7e-158b2aad5a2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6{x}^{2}-2xy}{{x}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336727} {:answer/id #uuid "4fa8641b-f12e-5282-8639-b287c9ca081d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6x-xy}{{x}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336764} {:answer/id #uuid "778b8f44-4e6f-5e84-b5d0-3856a65b2fcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6-2xy}{{x}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337107} {:answer/id #uuid "8e0a7e37-7922-5237-be4e-0d4daf2edd15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6x-2y}{{x}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337313} {:answer/id #uuid "d8ffdd28-a653-5368-a470-1763333eed55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6x+2xy}{{x}^{2}-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337920}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b322d9c-37ab-52f7-9c7e-158b2aad5a2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6{x}^{2}-2xy}{{x}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336727} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8ea9a9d-1024-53db-ae2f-47b539ab86b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338714}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3b84d5b9-93df-56c4-be18-4346c8b72764" :question/math-academy-id "q-72260" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for ${x}^{2}y+4y=2{x}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the ${x}^{2}y$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y+4y & =2{x}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}y+4y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}y)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}) \\\\ [{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\cdot 2x]+4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy+4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6{x}^{2}-2xy \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+4) & =6{x}^{2}-2xy \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{6{x}^{2}-2xy}{{x}^{2}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403550 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087822 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "79daf9dd-814d-562b-b3bc-b066bd2dfb90" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift Applied to an Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93a712bc-5c5d-5646-9703-82e77d69b46e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3({3}^{x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403552} {:answer/id #uuid "c63beba4-7686-58ae-845d-111254d3e6ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={3}^{x}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403553} {:answer/id #uuid "5996d30b-a352-5547-9b35-f09224ff8616" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={3}^{x}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403554} {:answer/id #uuid "e2d5db3c-106b-5a20-8512-215f226e23f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={3}^{x}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403555} {:answer/id #uuid "3d9e24e9-f8f1-50d2-a309-392b65ff2168" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2({3}^{x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403556}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2d5db3c-106b-5a20-8512-215f226e23f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={3}^{x}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403555} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f6e4f21-8993-51e5-a1c9-737356240b0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403551}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bbca085f-0b60-5f9a-bdc1-1da6491170fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-7230" :question/problem "![](images/87/8764149ca0000522277d48bf48f4ef14ba06f7e4793ba1a58ef639ff7af3a3b9.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y={3}^{x}+c.$ Determine the value of $c$ and find the equation of the curve." :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of $y={3}^{x}$ is $y=0.$ The vertical translation $y={3}^{x}+c$ shifts the asymptote to $y=c.$\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-2.$ Therefore, $c=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403557 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087822 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "79daf9dd-814d-562b-b3bc-b066bd2dfb90" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift Applied to an Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "760d4f2f-67c3-578b-90ce-e44b5cc0e475" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={2}^{x}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403559} {:answer/id #uuid "a42ff124-ce1b-58ec-a4c4-728cb4b536df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3({2}^{x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403560} {:answer/id #uuid "9bb95f56-7efa-56a4-ab5b-5f4220d4b140" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={2}^{x}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403561} {:answer/id #uuid "840799e0-019f-5e56-960e-e4dce9f6a43e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={3}^{x}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403562} {:answer/id #uuid "97c4002b-68c4-5b92-bb46-3a3c52c7bbac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3({2}^{x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403563}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9bb95f56-7efa-56a4-ab5b-5f4220d4b140" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={2}^{x}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403561} :answer-field/id #uuid "117f36b7-d2dd-58cf-81f2-744857f29ebf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403558}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bb2d08ab-7e1f-579d-8314-e037ebc00133" :question/math-academy-id "q-7232" :question/problem "![](images/2a/2ad72b3df34f32d6a76f79f6801887bd6fe1d762fefd74071517a754b1890ac2.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y={2}^{x}+c.$ Determine the value of $c$ and find the equation of the curve." :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of $y={2}^{x}$ is $y=0.$ The vertical translation $y={2}^{x}+c$ shifts the asymptote to $y=c.$\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-3.$ Therefore, $c=-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339090 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068690 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f29435f-b112-5999-b0ca-9003d9e622e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Indeterminate Limit of the Form Infinity Divided by Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2905b3c7-5120-5928-8659-ab7cf49215cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336415} {:answer/id #uuid "9c9f02de-6945-5986-9bd6-77c4fda53a38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337447} {:answer/id #uuid "c4c17bce-f321-5a5a-b8a2-e2f57aa0f0a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337781} {:answer/id #uuid "d1424795-2c4b-569b-984c-33e713fb9a2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337866} {:answer/id #uuid "f3baaf2a-856c-5b97-ba7e-56d8806079c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338162}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3baaf2a-856c-5b97-ba7e-56d8806079c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338162} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d342821-bbce-5fd1-b3bb-822b8446cf81" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338330}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "83ace1c9-0a10-59aa-bb99-7a86a6dbfa17" :question/math-academy-id "q-72333" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{1/x}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=-\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}{e}^{1/x}=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{-\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{1/x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]^{′}}{[{e}^{1/x}]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})}{{e}^{1/x}(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{x}^{-1}}{{e}^{1/x}\\cdot {x}^{-2}} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{x}{{e}^{1/x}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339092 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068690 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f29435f-b112-5999-b0ca-9003d9e622e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Indeterminate Limit of the Form Infinity Divided by Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dd6dd70-5f78-54b6-ac97-950f2f384aef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336473} {:answer/id #uuid "4e08260e-b4f6-5c7a-ad2d-8294f523a3ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336757} {:answer/id #uuid "5b538add-4209-5721-8b56-652729cc391f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336862} {:answer/id #uuid "7d7564a4-f738-5faf-a2c3-bdd625a0ef99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337163} {:answer/id #uuid "bb83b45c-700e-5a49-955e-7ea35708e865" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337697}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e08260e-b4f6-5c7a-ad2d-8294f523a3ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336757} :answer-field/id #uuid "64250102-7382-5959-9fea-4e5f4501809d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338454}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d0dae206-0030-50d7-a470-3e9d541986a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-72336" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1)}.$" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1)=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}]}^{′}}{{[\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})}{(-\\frac{4}{{x}^{5}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{-3}}{4{x}^{-5}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069507 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068690 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f29435f-b112-5999-b0ca-9003d9e622e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Indeterminate Limit of the Form Infinity Divided by Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c029288-0577-53f2-b622-20d182eb4958" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336446} {:answer/id #uuid "537b5f65-839c-5501-a32d-7c7e7a49a12b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336806} {:answer/id #uuid "77455b1e-3f23-5bec-b73a-1b6c9dc86ab2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337106} {:answer/id #uuid "9e26fb10-c993-50f5-8b93-429253440f15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337463} {:answer/id #uuid "e2592f07-77fc-5726-895f-b9958ca63d12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338000}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2592f07-77fc-5726-895f-b9958ca63d12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338000} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7f9d37f-e6cb-53f6-b39d-a73c1eb6742f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338656}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8a9948ce-563f-5b0f-bd31-1937130137d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-72339" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{-\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)]}^{′}}{[\\operatorname{csc}⁡x]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\cancel{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339100 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068722 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b89c50f3-2181-5b61-96cb-386ef88d2035" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Expression Involving a Power Function Multiplied by Its Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "181818f9-ee5d-570b-8805-3453bec6da26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{(1+{x}^{5})^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336257} {:answer/id #uuid "324b72d3-41e0-5026-af58-5b34dc0551ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{1+{x}^{5}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336509} {:answer/id #uuid "601c5f25-3385-59f3-b9ec-6f4783410687" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{5}}}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336901} {:answer/id #uuid "d13e9d9a-a682-5a93-b274-9657dd12c702" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x\\sqrt{1+{x}^{5}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337864} {:answer/id #uuid "e2d6ab9a-d942-55d2-8674-766bbfa15318" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{(1+{x}^{5})^{3}}}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338008}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "324b72d3-41e0-5026-af58-5b34dc0551ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{1+{x}^{5}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336509} :answer-field/id #uuid "1287170e-aae1-5d7e-9ef3-ec229bb6713f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338307}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "94a2c36c-7f20-5b89-950f-742f856d9127" :question/math-academy-id "q-72382" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{5{x}^{4}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We note that $5{x}^{4}$ is the derivative of the quantity in the radical. So we make a substitution\n\n$$\nu=1+{x}^{5}.\n$$\n\nIts derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =5{x}^{4}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=5{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand rewriting our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5{x}^{4}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{5}}}\\,5{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{1/2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\int {u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve the integral using the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{{u}^{1/2}}{(\\frac{1}{2})}+C \\\\  & =2{u}^{1/2}+C \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{u}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we substitute $u=1+{x}^{5}$ back in the expression. So the final result is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5{x}^{4}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\sqrt{1+{x}^{5}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339103 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087824 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b70a3b7-90a7-5a83-b71f-304f7f25c4ef" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift and Stretch Applied to an Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "121b16a2-0458-5600-9a6f-f4f6e552fd5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3({2}^{x})-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336202} {:answer/id #uuid "4c671da3-c302-5f3c-9ea5-2dc6dac2cc6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3({2}^{x})+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336742} {:answer/id #uuid "6fed05ea-acde-5be8-b414-01135dc6f042" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3({2}^{x})-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337023} {:answer/id #uuid "826ab4ce-caa8-5db7-9188-f1b3cd74a9bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={2}^{x}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337218} {:answer/id #uuid "9cf95245-48b9-524a-917b-0ba71da5cdb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2({2}^{x})-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337452}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fed05ea-acde-5be8-b414-01135dc6f042" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3({2}^{x})-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337023} :answer-field/id #uuid "0eab9c10-198f-58c5-918e-000cc777273e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338303}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9f0333a8-0cc0-5b0c-9999-f5175286f364" :question/math-academy-id "q-7239" :question/problem "![](images/1c/1ca11413bbce2ff307e494fb5a79028535b76cff24619e6921620f88c3e274ec.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k({2}^{x})+c.$  Determine the values of $k$ and $c$ and find the equation of the curve." :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k({2}^{x})+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-1,$ so $c=-1.$\n\nTherefore, our function takes the form\n\n$$\ny=k({2}^{x})-1.\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,2),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=2$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =k({2}^{x})-1 \\\\ 2 & =k({2}^{0})-1 \\\\ 2 & =k(1)-1 \\\\ 2 & =k-1 \\\\ 3 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curve is $y=3({2}^{x})-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366254 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129173 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8537cc22-8d18-57c8-9ebf-921bd4d06ae7" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Double Summation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8cdaccea-2f1f-579c-b237-2d5393055b73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366256} {:answer/id #uuid "aaa1a53e-523b-569f-83dd-047da5436dc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366257} {:answer/id #uuid "aabe863f-76d5-5c86-94b0-a588b897eac6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366258} {:answer/id #uuid "02cf9849-79e2-570a-93e1-9506dfe614b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366259} {:answer/id #uuid "4a8a378b-ad0f-59e3-b2db-2bb3d3c2a896" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366260}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02cf9849-79e2-570a-93e1-9506dfe614b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366259} :answer-field/id #uuid "11de832f-cbe6-5fad-ae01-189beccacca5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366255}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a4955833-cb02-5857-a78e-70ed3219424f" :question/math-academy-id "q-72396" :question/problem "${\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{3}(i-j)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write out the inner summation by summing over the index $j:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{3}(i-j) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}[{\\sum }_{j=1}^{3}(i-j)] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}((i-1)+(i-2)+(i-3)) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}(3i-6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we sum over the index $i:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}(3i-6) & =(3\\cdot 1-6)+(3\\cdot 2-6)+(3\\cdot 3-6) \\\\  & =-3+0+3 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339109 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087824 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b70a3b7-90a7-5a83-b71f-304f7f25c4ef" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift and Stretch Applied to an Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49005b9e-8885-518c-a503-38932dbc77f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1.5({2}^{x})+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336710} {:answer/id #uuid "a7777608-b4d4-5368-84cb-c7a407165b82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1.5({2}^{x})+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337546} {:answer/id #uuid "ba7873cf-81b9-5aaf-8051-9f548d554ab8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0.5({2}^{x})+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337684} {:answer/id #uuid "c3ad9d29-36d5-5b4e-bad4-d6181862fae0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1.5({2}^{x})+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337768} {:answer/id #uuid "e97102d4-c948-5475-a8f4-3282b09cc04f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0.5({2}^{x})+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338069}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49005b9e-8885-518c-a503-38932dbc77f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1.5({2}^{x})+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336710} :answer-field/id #uuid "08a06ece-0b13-5c0a-9459-301060485f8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338290}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5a92c523-6f37-5b9d-8ed7-2c5b4d7360ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-7241" :question/problem "![](images/3a/3a78c4fc61bbf60e1cdfdba6303ecc1a9d5e09caa21d0879556106893a931a09.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k({2}^{x})+c.$  Determine the values of $k$ and $c$ and find the equation of the curve." :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k({2}^{x})+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=4,$ so $c=4.$\n\nTherefore, our function takes the form\n\n$$\ny=k({2}^{x})+4.\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,5.5),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=5.5$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =k({2}^{x})+4 \\\\ 5.5 & =k({2}^{0})+4 \\\\ 5.5 & =k(1)+4 \\\\ 5.5 & =k+4 \\\\ 1.5 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curve is $y=1.5({2}^{x})+4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398961 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064203 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2b47545-fdf1-5e6a-b0bb-bbbecb8067cc" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Algebraic Function With Positive Integer Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6058cb14-4722-5ae2-982a-1d1d72759305" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(7-2x)^{8}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398963} {:answer/id #uuid "615ab3c3-1cc9-56db-a561-9ade7641ace4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}(7-2x)^{8}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398964} {:answer/id #uuid "2b200597-1f86-5ecd-b8bc-18b09c132aad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(7-2x)^{8}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398965} {:answer/id #uuid "13a3fa38-b420-5956-8a4b-2194f2d8ef30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}(7-2x)^{8}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398966} {:answer/id #uuid "5674f007-df38-5b04-ac90-1342c70182a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(7-2x)^{8}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398967}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6058cb14-4722-5ae2-982a-1d1d72759305" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(7-2x)^{8}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398963} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ee57767-a470-5ed2-888e-e7479cb9accb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398962}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8c75bb9c-44e2-5f73-9a68-3c296ae237a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-72424" :question/problem "$\\int 16(7-2x)^{7}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=7-2x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 16(7-2x)^{7}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 16{u}^{7}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =16\\int {u}^{7}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =16\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})\\int {u}^{7}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-8\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{8}}{8}+C \\\\  & =-{u}^{8}+C \\\\  & =-(7-2x)^{8}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[-(7-2x)^{8}+C] & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(7-2x)^{8}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-8(7-2x)^{7}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7-2x)+0 \\\\  & =-8(7-2x)^{7}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =16(7-2x)^{7}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339116 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064207 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bdbfa506-08a1-5e43-b958-2ae373df775d" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Linear Rational Function and Simplifying the Arbitrary Constant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ea8c3ea-0518-506a-9945-ef3bf2bf7936" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|7-x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336171} {:answer/id #uuid "6ae31738-7e11-5b61-bda5-73b51a186dc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{7}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|7-x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336978} {:answer/id #uuid "9dd74f15-1558-5167-ae1a-0738b496e927" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|7-x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337459} {:answer/id #uuid "afeaa9dd-27fb-515f-9b9a-a9fc160293f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|7-x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337612} {:answer/id #uuid "c8d07741-d286-59ef-b0bc-34120b2124b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|7-x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337807}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ea8c3ea-0518-506a-9945-ef3bf2bf7936" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|7-x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336171} :answer-field/id #uuid "14072edb-e48a-597d-a3df-afce4caaaa51" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338311}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b16baf99-001c-5b72-bc61-2ffc6c75b449" :question/math-academy-id "q-72445" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{7-x}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant." :question/worked-solution "First, let $u=7-x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=-\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u,$ solve it, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{7-x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (-1)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|7-x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can further simplify the above by writing $C=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡K$ for another arbitrary constant $K,$ and then we can combine the two terms using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|7-x|+C & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|7-x|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|7-x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡K) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|7-x|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we finally conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{7-x}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|7-x|).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339117 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064207 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bdbfa506-08a1-5e43-b958-2ae373df775d" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Linear Rational Function and Simplifying the Arbitrary Constant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07b76063-8fda-596f-8106-cbb57f2ef69a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|5-2x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336093} {:answer/id #uuid "86f6a2e9-4d29-5e23-9617-09660280aaa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|5-2x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337252} {:answer/id #uuid "b4ead48b-c59a-570e-a7de-39bcbc22b8fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|5-2x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337638} {:answer/id #uuid "bec88b66-0b15-559a-8f7c-3341c51c2ee0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|5-2x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337729} {:answer/id #uuid "f6322442-f9d7-5f7c-ac2d-896d214f1436" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|5-2x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338188}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bec88b66-0b15-559a-8f7c-3341c51c2ee0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|5-2x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337729} :answer-field/id #uuid "209f233a-25a9-55a5-ab33-40122ec3bf02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338337}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2d475585-492e-5142-bfa4-12e97136e061" :question/math-academy-id "q-72446" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{5-2x}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant." :question/worked-solution "First, let $u=5-2x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u,$ solve it, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{5-2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5-2x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can further simplify the above by writing $C=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K$ for another arbitrary constant $K,$ and then we can combine the two terms using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5-2x|+C & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5-2x|-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5-2x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡K) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|5-2x|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we finally conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{5-2x}\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|5-2x|).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339119 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064208 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "af32f07f-fa65-5ede-a506-735dc2d3a5ef" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Combination of Linear Rational Functions Using the General Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f5cd12b-daf8-57b4-a508-5b25997dfab6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|(2x+1)(2x-1)|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336323} {:answer/id #uuid "4b0ccfc1-4a8e-5fe1-b80c-9d8713467d76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|\\frac{2x+1}{2x-1}|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336723} {:answer/id #uuid "4e0854d1-efd5-57d5-ad92-34e8d95cd84e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|(2x+1)(2x-1)|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336758} {:answer/id #uuid "89828476-cdcf-5b30-b125-067c9a0a1d66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|\\frac{2x+1}{2x-1}|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337271} {:answer/id #uuid "8e9493db-04ea-5567-b436-e98702a20a0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|\\frac{2x+1}{2x-1}|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337316}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f5cd12b-daf8-57b4-a508-5b25997dfab6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|(2x+1)(2x-1)|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336323} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd143701-6720-5620-9d38-200cfec896ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338665}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b89e488e-b744-5dec-bdcd-c1a1eb5f6a8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-72449" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int (\\frac{1}{2x+1}+\\frac{1}{2x-1})\\,\\text{d}x.$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant." :question/worked-solution "Let's solve this using the general formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{1}{2x+1}+\\frac{1}{2x-1})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{2x-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(2x+1)(2x-1)|+\\frac{1}{2}\\text{ }\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|(2x+1)(2x-1)|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the laws of logarithms to write the result as a single logarithm."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339123 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068723 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8fe2a279-0b92-561e-b17d-b902f43725ac" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Expression Involving a Power Function Multiplied by a Constant Multiple of Its Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e59935f-f93b-599b-9816-d15f61104595" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{({x}^{2}-5)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336167} {:answer/id #uuid "1ccce204-ca7a-5eee-bc02-3fcc7e16e632" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{({x}^{2}-5)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336299} {:answer/id #uuid "4b5b5f53-8881-5e03-8cde-ddfcc41895a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{({x}^{2}-5)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336732} {:answer/id #uuid "75e7362c-a6a5-5145-bf5d-1527db0f317d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{({x}^{2}-5)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337095} {:answer/id #uuid "7bc5cfb0-4b70-528a-a0e4-e88e561188b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{({x}^{2}-5)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337152}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75e7362c-a6a5-5145-bf5d-1527db0f317d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{({x}^{2}-5)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337095} :answer-field/id #uuid "80b5679a-f4f7-56bb-9b5b-253ee5665aec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338504}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "de70203f-dd05-5f0d-9800-283717012618" :question/math-academy-id "q-72467" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-5}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, the $x$ that's outside the square root isn't quite the derivative of $({x}^{2}-5).$ But the derivative is $2x,$ so we're only out by a constant factor.\n\nSo we make the substitution\n\n$$\nu={x}^{2}-5.\n$$\n\nIts derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =2x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=x\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we can now write our integral in terms of $u,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-5}\\cdot x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int {u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve the integral in terms of $u$ and then write the final result in terms of $x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\int {u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{3/2}}{(\\frac{3}{2})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {u}^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{({x}^{2}-5)^{3}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111390 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110669 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34645eb8-3624-5ff3-817d-28f8d0cee4ba" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Scaling Constant From a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dc7ed7d-8822-50d6-adbe-ad3f4f94cc16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336309} {:answer/id #uuid "2855b2b9-6a8b-51fc-bc86-192bc1d62f6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336410} {:answer/id #uuid "62f307be-5f4b-5771-b9ee-77edd782e456" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336922} {:answer/id #uuid "75294229-d638-5281-9b75-127f796a0330" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337087} {:answer/id #uuid "a40c3d37-1f3e-56af-9259-897cf2fc43fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337522}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1dc7ed7d-8822-50d6-adbe-ad3f4f94cc16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336309} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfe47395-e6a4-5174-8bac-b9841d8af59f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338671}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7ca56a13-e2a8-587b-a791-0b4526ed9f52" :question/math-academy-id "q-7249" :question/problem "![](images/db/db4c6694a3bc172aea8a47f1a828eac8235286b7ce6b7e327574790a6c31c270.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}.$ What is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot the graph of $y=k{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}$ we take the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}$ and stretch it vertically by a scale factor of  $k.$ Under the action of this transformation, the $y$-intercept is mapped from $(0,1)$ to $(0,k).$\n\nComparing the point $(0,3)$ on the graph to $(0,k),$ we see that we must have $k=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403578 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129114 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d390841-351e-54f5-ad19-90b6ebae5799" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Matrices in Row Echelon Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "037eeb73-f010-5239-b320-397bc7facb77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403580} {:answer/id #uuid "48b44963-1902-5c0d-9522-c534c3678776" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403581} {:answer/id #uuid "0eeb9a23-f447-585f-9585-f82e287918e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403582} {:answer/id #uuid "c1989825-0208-5721-8305-7b2993765c83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403583} {:answer/id #uuid "610ceecf-c4a9-5885-8266-6104d4f37cf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403584}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "037eeb73-f010-5239-b320-397bc7facb77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403580} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8c3e5f4-9bc1-505b-a784-0a6ba8cc7e8a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403579}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "307a31ed-3320-5bc6-9033-1067b69032de" :question/math-academy-id "q-72491" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are **not** in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 5 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -7 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a matrix is in row echelon form if it satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is not in row echelon form since the leading entry ${a}_{43}\\ne 0$ (condition II is violated). \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is not in row echelon form since the leading entry ${c}_{32}\\ne 0$ (condition II is violated). \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 5 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -7 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $A$ and $C$ are **not** in row echelon form."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403585 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129114 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d390841-351e-54f5-ad19-90b6ebae5799" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Matrices in Row Echelon Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2449f17-fb24-5f21-b93a-91da7cb73b6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403587} {:answer/id #uuid "f82dd2a1-af9f-5176-9521-a602fb86b66c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403588} {:answer/id #uuid "a73d0fd1-33f8-5fef-91ee-18ea7bc00233" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403589} {:answer/id #uuid "1e393d24-d14c-55d4-9c1c-d1fb4487bb44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403590} {:answer/id #uuid "0a24998d-88d6-567d-b060-eecb883f1a8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403591}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e393d24-d14c-55d4-9c1c-d1fb4487bb44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403590} :answer-field/id #uuid "80541a17-4cbb-5d55-a794-14a52b470ccb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403586}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ba6bcd83-87df-5c0b-b121-63a0ff1205ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-72492" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are **not** in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a matrix is in row echelon form if it satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is not in row echelon form since the leading entry ${c}_{22}\\ne 0$ (condition II is violated). \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $C$ is **not** in row echelon form."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339129 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110599 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29005c66-9f30-5862-926f-8a09320d404c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Lines in Slope-Intercept Form are Perpendicular"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad7f19f7-5593-5372-821e-d1252d3b8446" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337587} {:answer/id #uuid "c0e66778-1d33-59c9-9c63-84193616625d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337745} {:answer/id #uuid "cf4a56a8-6d0c-5363-9d42-b65beb462d50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{x}{2}x+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337846} {:answer/id #uuid "cf58d7b6-9e04-5b2a-b15f-7c5224686dfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{x}{2}x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337847} {:answer/id #uuid "f9e1b841-fe74-5a6f-9da0-dd60389da801" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x+19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338222}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9e1b841-fe74-5a6f-9da0-dd60389da801" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x+19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338222} :answer-field/id #uuid "833a989b-8bb1-589d-92ce-6ba9062090a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338511}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cdd0bbdf-150a-52db-a038-e888ce1f0809" :question/math-academy-id "q-72524" :question/problem "Which of the following lines is perpendicular to $y=-\\frac{x}{2}+7?$" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nThe line $y=-\\frac{x}{2}+7$ is in slope-intercept form, and its slope is $-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y=2x+19$ has a slope of $2.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y=2x+19$ is perpendicular to the given line."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403592 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068900 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "66ade855-4b16-514b-b44a-1d6b9d61ef28" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift Applied to an Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "128902a2-01ba-574b-8cb8-9e24d91588d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403594} {:answer/id #uuid "d421b62c-91bc-51bd-9a1e-dcb674541fa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403595} {:answer/id #uuid "89b95e28-4bdc-577f-9fef-39eb015deed9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403596} {:answer/id #uuid "1f9a6792-46dd-54a0-852a-c3ce900255d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403597} {:answer/id #uuid "cdf02042-68e8-57bc-821d-c4e709f6e6d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403598}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f9a6792-46dd-54a0-852a-c3ce900255d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403597} :answer-field/id #uuid "86226cb6-da89-5070-bdaf-be3482566831" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403593}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c80543b3-4997-5175-82f2-660b5af23e52" :question/math-academy-id "q-7253" :question/problem "![](images/50/50be1f4a894e86b5f06fba18655caca163c3d4c7a3423d2ecf159aa49975eb8c.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+c.$ Determine the value of $c$ and find the equation of the curve." :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of $y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}$ is $y=0.$\n\nThe vertical translation $y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+c$ shifts the asymptote to $y=c.$\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=1.$ Therefore, $c=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069509 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068724 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "18ce1807-3a09-5ebd-b3cd-a91db8d2b4a8" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Rational Function Using Natural Logarithms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a56f775-026e-59fc-b647-34d85fe8bf61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3{x}^{2}-1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336275} {:answer/id #uuid "a1728457-38ba-5bbb-927c-7282a90dc1b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{3}-1|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337496} {:answer/id #uuid "b51f8e66-052a-5029-8ff7-97299bdc0642" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{3}-x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337639} {:answer/id #uuid "f743a007-0c6c-5682-a772-447548f069d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3{x}^{2}-x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338200} {:answer/id #uuid "f9436168-b71c-5730-9e91-324b6cdb60f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}-1}|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338217}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b51f8e66-052a-5029-8ff7-97299bdc0642" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{3}-x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337639} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9839270-94f7-5e94-999d-41a70f88e25f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338685}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "604f6b09-192e-50e6-8477-dce586313508" :question/math-academy-id "q-72566" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}-1}{{x}^{3}-x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u={x}^{3}-x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}-1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=(3{x}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nand we can solve the resulting integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}-1}{{x}^{3}-x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}-x}\\cdot (3{x}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{3}-x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341834 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175923 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "44b9b212-27c4-5d6d-a221-cd85c25fc956" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Line Perpendicular to a Line Through Two Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "460f3e7e-a692-5e84-84d3-bb04895e42c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x+9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339750} {:answer/id #uuid "7de51bcc-dba4-5f66-9ed1-e89e2285349b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340264} {:answer/id #uuid "a496b65e-0fa0-5379-bb60-a181cb54a9be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}x+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340623} {:answer/id #uuid "dc1a70a6-b427-555b-b378-aaf49435f770" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341084} {:answer/id #uuid "f252e1db-2d4f-5cfe-b195-696e6f606c03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341262}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a496b65e-0fa0-5379-bb60-a181cb54a9be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}x+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340623} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4e67ea3-0bdd-589c-834c-8bb372afcf1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "99fe5e6a-20b3-53d0-a013-c1c2c0d3ad0c" :question/math-academy-id "q-72568" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(-2,5)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(6,1)$ and $(4,5).$" :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes which are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(6,1)$ and $(4,5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5-1}{4-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{-2} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (-2)=-1\\,⇒\\,m=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(-2,5).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-5 & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-(-2)) \\\\ y-5 & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+2) \\\\ y-5 & =\\frac{1}{2}x+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341836 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175924 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c52fd5dd-780e-5dab-805f-561383873dd4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Line Perpendicular to a Line in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e1988b7-4bc8-5bda-86c5-639e0552dedd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-3y=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339683} {:answer/id #uuid "51dac7ff-2d97-5661-b543-ad769758a707" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339880} {:answer/id #uuid "6d11b816-6f21-575c-a776-01785873cb84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-3y=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340089} {:answer/id #uuid "803a7a88-7553-50e1-862d-0b786cfc737c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+3y=13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340279} {:answer/id #uuid "af6755f9-b09b-562d-a589-5067ac2ec549" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+3y=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340728}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "803a7a88-7553-50e1-862d-0b786cfc737c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+3y=13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340279} :answer-field/id #uuid "58e7ea68-a247-56ce-a08d-252af6a5df70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341536}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ad191a02-5856-5306-8980-ab9514c55d3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-72571" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(5,1)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(1,5)$ and $(-3,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes which are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(1,5)$ and $(-3,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1-5}{-3-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6}{-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2})=-1\\,⇒\\,m=-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(5,1).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{2}{3}(x-5) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{10}{3} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{13}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we want to write the equation in standard form. So, we will bring the $x$ term to the left-hand side and multiply both sides of the equation by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{13}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}x+y & =\\frac{13}{3} \\\\ 3\\cdot (\\frac{2}{3}x+y) & =3\\cdot \\frac{13}{3} \\\\ 2x+3y & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405146 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129113 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5d1d859-0b86-5507-a7bf-3d805a85adf3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Augmented Matrices in Row Echelon Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9415a6bc-b845-5416-a630-c09cf1de4075" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405148} {:answer/id #uuid "d774e169-e628-59dd-b3d3-32eef4cc221b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405149} {:answer/id #uuid "820bcbf2-b300-5622-88e4-cea1d1927af8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405150} {:answer/id #uuid "6a49ee16-b14b-5b39-9515-f019516811a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405151} {:answer/id #uuid "47b78352-073a-55e3-977a-ffd9d1cb0ba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405152}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47b78352-073a-55e3-977a-ffd9d1cb0ba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405152} :answer-field/id #uuid "cca85be9-de3b-5573-9094-83fec95f1da6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405147}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bbb2727c-01c4-5345-9e38-1d38c7fb43da" :question/math-academy-id "q-72575" :question/problem "Which of the following augmented matrices have coefficient matrices that are **not** in row echelon form? Treat the final column of each augmented matrix as its constants column.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 4 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 5 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 0 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A coefficient matrix, obtained by omitting the final column of its augmented matrix, is in row echelon form if it satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The coefficient matrix of $A$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The coefficient matrix of $B$ is not in row echelon form since the leading entries of its first two rows are in columns $3$ and $2,$ respectively (condition II is violated).\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 4 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 5 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The coefficient matrix of $C$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 0 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $B$ has a coefficient matrix that is **not** in row echelon form."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341840 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068724 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "18ce1807-3a09-5ebd-b3cd-a91db8d2b4a8" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Rational Function Using Natural Logarithms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "424f1750-2255-5cd3-97f1-bff8ae1f779e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2-5{x}^{5}|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339710} {:answer/id #uuid "64a24bd8-02d1-5da1-875a-ff8313d79449" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5(2-5{x}^{5})^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340018} {:answer/id #uuid "cd66158f-066f-573b-a023-86fcc42573cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2(2-5{x}^{5})^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340950} {:answer/id #uuid "d40a6f74-0d5f-5192-b1f2-24adfd672a83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2-5{x}^{5}|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341003} {:answer/id #uuid "f1c87e78-2256-558f-a463-3af7ee7e05a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2-5{x}^{5}|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1c87e78-2256-558f-a463-3af7ee7e05a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2-5{x}^{5}|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341259} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4f33047-2f0b-50bf-84ac-e403b1fabf2c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341723}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "958efc2b-d91b-53e5-b712-1690bc1b7068" :question/math-academy-id "q-72605" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{10{x}^{4}}{2-5{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=2-5{x}^{5}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-25{x}^{4}\\,⟹\\,(-\\frac{1}{25})\\text{d}u={x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nand we can solve the resulting integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{10{x}^{4}}{2-5{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x & =10\\int \\frac{1}{2-5{x}^{5}}\\cdot {x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{25})\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{25}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2-5{x}^{5}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341843 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087768 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3520d323-c6a4-5bce-bac1-1f4c02bd0068" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Circle Given the Center and a Point on the Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44ceba2e-461c-5d6f-94d0-fffabcaa7f4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}=\\sqrt{41}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339737} {:answer/id #uuid "67364410-21ff-5ab6-b499-984469e0f0b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=41" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340042} {:answer/id #uuid "7e6b7a0c-3051-526a-9636-efd77cb0b591" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+3)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=41" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340268} {:answer/id #uuid "c00a31e7-30ad-5958-a723-a2568cb7ceb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=\\sqrt{41}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340844} {:answer/id #uuid "ccdecbe3-6add-5929-86dd-7b81307f2bae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+3)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=\\sqrt{41}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340941}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67364410-21ff-5ab6-b499-984469e0f0b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=41" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340042} :answer-field/id #uuid "59370454-96d7-5c69-8869-e408beefd110" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341537}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b538b794-53e2-55d6-941e-f772138f1dd6" :question/math-academy-id "q-72621" :question/problem "![](images/6e/6e68ede9db2528ea607fc6fd63f0ec4ca1c1378982239913ef49153e1cf5b609.png)\n\nThe circle above is centered at $C(3,-3)$ and the point $X(-1,2)$ lies on the circumference. Find the equation of the circle." :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe length of the radius is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CX & =\\sqrt{{(-1-3)}^{2}+{(2-(-3))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{41}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our circle is generated by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(y-(-3))^{2} & =(\\sqrt{41})^{2} \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =41\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341845 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059641 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3332b534-bd04-5e86-995e-1e6d691d8e1b" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression by Factoring and Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "733d9013-5828-5e8a-a6ca-98d0ee84db3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340151} {:answer/id #uuid "9e08cb06-fa94-559a-b45f-0247593ae993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340553} {:answer/id #uuid "ce427bad-7fbf-553d-94e7-4eac1f2a458e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340960} {:answer/id #uuid "d512d53f-d1ef-5fcf-a459-a5ecee4d6396" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341012} {:answer/id #uuid "ddb559b7-bf25-5a4d-8461-b1784d7b4dd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341097}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d512d53f-d1ef-5fcf-a459-a5ecee4d6396" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341012} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c77d87a-34f1-5f64-8f04-06a22766d6be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341597}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5b1e203a-fce6-56f0-bd3a-5a969f3f611d" :question/math-academy-id "q-72711" :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2{cos}^{3}⁡x+2{sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the expression:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2{cos}^{3}⁡x+2{sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x({cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x)\n$$\n\nThen, we use ${cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x=1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x({cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2{cos}^{3}⁡x+2{sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341846 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059642 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "868125f0-22e0-57fc-9620-f3242419a689" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying a Rational Expression Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18d5cf9e-61a5-56dc-ab85-48cc3361b6cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339348} {:answer/id #uuid "2a4a8bfb-24ab-5d4f-85c0-b03c7be1078c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339502} {:answer/id #uuid "5af65e91-2dda-5a02-a6b9-84e591e12cdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339956} {:answer/id #uuid "94adee08-1ae4-5711-87c2-0b92afcedb4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340463} {:answer/id #uuid "a2128603-79a4-5ac2-aad8-d0e79ce06174" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94adee08-1ae4-5711-87c2-0b92afcedb4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340463} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ffbbed8-ad51-5238-b319-cf240d8126f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "508dcfe6-ec7a-5fa0-ab23-63e62cd88ad5" :question/math-academy-id "q-72712" :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\frac{{sin}^{3}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x+1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ from the first two terms:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{sin}^{3}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x+1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)+1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)+1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{sin}^{3}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x+1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385557 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119775 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74764043-e1e7-56d6-8f53-9d287a30e538" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant After Switching Two Rows"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07c9312f-a209-5f87-ba1c-5386579d3bd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "69" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385559} {:answer/id #uuid "66f9fe22-3a07-5c23-9891-3cd2ed9aaf28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-69" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385560} {:answer/id #uuid "021f7c92-ef0d-5de9-bbfa-11ad3cbd3683" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385561} {:answer/id #uuid "ccfef29b-6d70-59f9-a92f-9b34777975d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385562} {:answer/id #uuid "eb51bd1c-5c18-5345-9235-64937bfb3725" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385563}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07c9312f-a209-5f87-ba1c-5386579d3bd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "69" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385559} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5c1ce63-8b9e-5445-9f07-9e20c1f3be11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385558}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "457d76f4-06af-5356-9e00-91c260ff5a27" :question/math-academy-id "q-72725" :question/problem "Suppose $C$ is a $9\\times 9$ matrix and $det(C)=-69.$ The matrix $D$ is obtained from $C$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{5}↔{R}_{8}$ (switching the fifth and the eighth rows). What is the value of $det(D)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since swapping any two rows changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite sign, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =-det(C) \\\\  & =-(-69) \\\\  & =69.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393215 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092184 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4691976a-d825-5f6c-aed7-0d9b45560952" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 2x2 System of Equations Using an Inverse Matrix With Reorganization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40c24857-06a2-5e0b-85d2-5f2b717c9839" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393217} {:answer/id #uuid "54b091df-4df8-574b-ab7b-0a3fde77dee4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393218} {:answer/id #uuid "30420fca-ad93-57fd-ba3b-06ad555ade16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393219} {:answer/id #uuid "e0dddfb6-deca-5831-988a-cc662b273194" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393220} {:answer/id #uuid "4d2b60cb-753e-5c81-be84-11c5779434a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393221}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30420fca-ad93-57fd-ba3b-06ad555ade16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393219} :answer-field/id #uuid "b977679f-6256-57c2-8887-0c386f7a473b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393216}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cf30e2dd-fc54-551b-b230-ad61e11fe0db" :question/math-academy-id "q-72726" :question/problem "Which of the following represents the solution to the system of linear equations given below?\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3x-y=4 \\\\ x=-2y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3x-y=4 \\\\ x+2y=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the solution to this system as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341852 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092184 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4691976a-d825-5f6c-aed7-0d9b45560952" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 2x2 System of Equations Using an Inverse Matrix With Reorganization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cae29bf-6657-5948-b4ac-de9d15a83b80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339243} {:answer/id #uuid "1c79ef91-d60f-5461-9182-a09de75ab44e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339373} {:answer/id #uuid "50658568-e9ab-5946-9ac1-0eb64885c225" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339861} {:answer/id #uuid "7a038809-9690-59a5-9f4d-8538600ea5bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340216} {:answer/id #uuid "af86119f-4c68-5d25-8752-90c8805840da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340730}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af86119f-4c68-5d25-8752-90c8805840da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340730} :answer-field/id #uuid "39ce73d2-08ab-546f-9ccf-5116afbbd6e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341484}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ae55f4eb-c009-586b-a5eb-f77f0f124cd0" :question/math-academy-id "q-72733" :question/problem "Which of the following represents the solution to the system of linear equations given below?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x=y+1 \\\\ x-y=2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x-y=1 \\\\ x-y=2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{(-1)\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393222 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059772 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c27a3f3c-77c7-55e9-8714-1f24c4a48063" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Row Operations To an Augmented Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6756ed00-330f-5c8c-8a4d-fbb86cdc2c8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393224} {:answer/id #uuid "8dfcb35e-95c9-5403-a320-dcd7face84d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393225} {:answer/id #uuid "d3557d97-0c1d-5853-b9fd-91ee81522e54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393226} {:answer/id #uuid "7936a577-d3bd-55bd-b9c4-bfcbd8d64f15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393227} {:answer/id #uuid "3c473b8d-8970-5a89-afb6-b17390deb661" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393228}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c473b8d-8970-5a89-afb6-b17390deb661" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393228} :answer-field/id #uuid "43860d4a-16fb-54d3-8d76-5164ce10d9b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393223}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7e529998-60b8-53c0-bae5-50351fb55c46" :question/math-academy-id "q-72757" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}$ on $T?$" :question/worked-solution "The operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}$ means that we need to add $2$ times the first row to the second row. \n\nApplying this row operation, we obtain the following:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ -2+(2)(1) & -2+(2)(3) & 1+(2)(2)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341858 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105906 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5dd3dcff-06d2-5d61-9863-0f5b541d8d4b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area of a Triangle Given the Area of a Quadrilateral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f07a6b5-32ea-529d-9160-238820145dc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34{\\text{ cm}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339267} {:answer/id #uuid "174b9032-2956-564a-826e-c2c81592003e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23{\\text{ cm}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339336} {:answer/id #uuid "508acfdf-0761-5691-be12-cc8ecd53beb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19{\\text{ cm}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339862} {:answer/id #uuid "84059e1d-50ea-57c1-a02c-e73cba67744f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13{\\text{ cm}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340317} {:answer/id #uuid "f79db4a6-d5f5-5ae3-9697-f4ab3f3df76c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25{\\text{ cm}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341318}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "508acfdf-0761-5691-be12-cc8ecd53beb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19{\\text{ cm}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339862} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea1b3e9c-7be4-50dc-84b7-55a05c94254f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341796}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d86eb6a4-4303-50bf-b47a-fa898e3f8037" :question/math-academy-id "q-72764" :question/problem "![](images/f5/f53996727138b9442ac041cf492618db8287b54ba652c4f9e375cdde27dc1862.png)\n\nThe area of $ABCD$ is $34{\\text{ cm}}^{2}.$   What is the area of $△BCD$ rounded to the nearest integer?" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by finding the area of $△ABD.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△ABD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot AB\\cdot AD\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 9\\cdot 15\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{52}^{∘} \\\\  & =67.5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{52}^{∘} \\\\  & =53.190… \\\\  & ≈53{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nThe difference of the areas of $△ABD$ and the quadrilateral $ABCD$ gives the area of $△BCD.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△BCD} & ={A}_{△ABD}-{A}_{ABCD} \\\\  & =53-34 \\\\  & =19{\\text{ cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341859 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105905 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3173d361-b91d-5699-97d8-6595ef59d0a3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area of a Quadrilateral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d011d15-855e-593b-a305-503c6f4d7b4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339829} {:answer/id #uuid "5049855a-0cf1-52ff-a6d7-82ad580a1998" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "135" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339858} {:answer/id #uuid "65970e9b-6b55-55f9-87cb-642a2bfcf59a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "132" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340026} {:answer/id #uuid "810e57dc-6211-51e2-9530-7c30c274a5a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340286} {:answer/id #uuid "941f7586-f901-520c-ba5a-d3f77fa4895c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "112" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340460}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5049855a-0cf1-52ff-a6d7-82ad580a1998" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "135" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339858} :answer-field/id #uuid "59a7e60a-6a2a-50ed-b8ee-1ebc1c309e07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341540}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c1e965d5-3d0b-5c28-9671-3eb1e1598d0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-72768" :question/problem "![](images/36/36af7324fd2112d94feddc5a1a02a91d97bbc0866cb93a6ca711dd73f566305a.png)\n\nFind, to the nearest integer, the area of the parallelogram $ABCD.$" :question/worked-solution "The diagonal $\\overset{―}{AC}$ divides the parallelogram into two congruent triangles. Therefore, the area of the parallelogram is twice the area of $△ACB$ (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/7c/7cfcb5cdf77b4afbf25afd64e37bb33f0ab2c211e09659181bfc16db0ccd2db7.png)\n\n Computing the area of $△ACB,$ we find\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ACD} & =\\frac{1}{2}(18)(8)\\operatorname{sin}⁡{70}^{∘} \\\\  & =72\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{70}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈67.658,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places. Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABCD} & =2{A}_{ACB} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 67.658 \\\\  & =135.316.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to the nearest integer, the area of the parallelogram is $135.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393236 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059772 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c27a3f3c-77c7-55e9-8714-1f24c4a48063" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Row Operations To an Augmented Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "756e234a-8217-5c8b-abaa-69ea121a0c6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 7 & -1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393238} {:answer/id #uuid "f11a15bf-be0f-553b-b1be-80f41c622717" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 7 & -1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393239} {:answer/id #uuid "1a9cf17b-a149-5bc6-9345-792b1cc2a3c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 7 & -1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393240} {:answer/id #uuid "1d4576b0-5970-52fb-8377-b193cb931a98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 7 & -1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393241} {:answer/id #uuid "4cfa023b-7905-5d00-83b5-528df496267c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 7 & -1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393242}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f11a15bf-be0f-553b-b1be-80f41c622717" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 7 & -1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393239} :answer-field/id #uuid "bdfc7f8f-fcd9-55fe-82a8-5f2ff5b50086" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393237}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2facacda-0611-5507-a35e-091ce7069bfa" :question/math-academy-id "q-72781" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $N,$ given by\n\n$$\nN=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 7 & -1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ on $N?$" :question/worked-solution "The operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ means that we need to add $-2$ times the first row to the second row.\n\nApplying this row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 7 & -1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2)\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2+(-2)(1) & 1+(-2)(-2) & 2+(-2)(1) & 3+(-2)(-1) \\\\ 7 & -1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 7 & -1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064950 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064262 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c234eb8-4a5b-52e1-8854-c183b68184bd" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Position of a Particle Given Its Velocity and Initial Position"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4388ef43-fe94-5421-acdd-c6a1590a53cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=9t+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339720} {:answer/id #uuid "80ca8a93-66a2-59da-a40a-1d062afa9efe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=9t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340283} {:answer/id #uuid "83eb9448-9014-55fc-b978-85a3dd0f45d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=9t-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340316} {:answer/id #uuid "9bc13499-3b34-59c1-a63a-2b6b94f92a5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340529} {:answer/id #uuid "a8c202e1-a092-59ce-aee9-f2474d57754f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340663}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83eb9448-9014-55fc-b978-85a3dd0f45d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=9t-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340316} :answer-field/id #uuid "f90189a6-f1b9-5e5c-97ab-aa0c39581484" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341823}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4bcd3d6d-85e2-53be-bfd3-fe166f94d8e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-7282" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t)=9,$ where $t$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x=0$ when $t=1,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t>0.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int 9\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =9t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $x(1)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =9(1)+C \\\\ C & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=9t-9.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341864 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092167 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8c7ab50-5a8b-5bcd-8483-cec8bf6bd3ce" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Tangent with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ad93dd9-f865-5592-811e-14f384c64bbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339219} {:answer/id #uuid "31074855-d3da-586d-8c04-526c3ec62d18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339558} {:answer/id #uuid "3342aa60-3377-5de6-abd0-ce1b0d538d0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339583} {:answer/id #uuid "86a98dfc-6975-5761-a79b-d41b66fce8b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340339} {:answer/id #uuid "a3814b4e-90bf-5084-b757-74d52e377f50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340610}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ad93dd9-f865-5592-811e-14f384c64bbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339219} :answer-field/id #uuid "c29068c9-aa1b-5e85-b568-8a54965ad3b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341718}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5bb6f7b5-fd38-5f44-84f6-733c5b1973e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-72824" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-8$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to one decimal place?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-8)=-1.446\\,4…≈-1.446\\,4.\n$$\n\nSince $-1.446\\,4$ lies outside the given domain, it is not a solution.\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\pi -1.446\\,4≈1.695\\,2, \\\\ {x}_{2} & =2\\pi -1.446\\,4≈4.836\\,8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}≈1.695\\,2\\cdot 4.836\\,8≈8.2\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341866 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064262 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c234eb8-4a5b-52e1-8854-c183b68184bd" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Position of a Particle Given Its Velocity and Initial Position"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "030f864c-4fe8-549d-af96-e97726443f51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=6t+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339151} {:answer/id #uuid "0e6e0da6-0630-5e05-b1c9-496620bcd938" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={t}^{3}+{t}^{2}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339260} {:answer/id #uuid "50eb5854-e7b4-5074-9585-6b0b878655a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={t}^{3}+{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339867} {:answer/id #uuid "98225b29-e168-596d-a3e1-abe2a661e410" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=4t+7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340495} {:answer/id #uuid "a662f1f6-40d3-5f4e-9954-53f79686f9e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={t}^{3}+{t}^{2}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340639}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a662f1f6-40d3-5f4e-9954-53f79686f9e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={t}^{3}+{t}^{2}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340639} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bad20ca-f454-584c-b7a3-1bab375f050a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341401}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "172f42b8-4e20-57a1-8447-38d9c7f98da1" :question/math-academy-id "q-7283" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t)=3{t}^{2}+2t,$ where $t$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x=15$ when $t=2,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t>0.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (3{t}^{2}+2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{3}+{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $x(2)=15.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15 & =(2)^{3}+(2)^{2}+C \\\\ 15 & =8+4+C \\\\ C & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)={t}^{3}+{t}^{2}+3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341870 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092171 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f607558-5a7f-52df-b344-57f2f84791e9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cosine with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "416a58ae-ce76-5e02-96f0-d7597149753a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339708} {:answer/id #uuid "6db8cad2-a957-58f3-ad26-db45b399e754" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340098} {:answer/id #uuid "a1cd8970-9cb3-5318-9504-b8c8a617dc91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340596} {:answer/id #uuid "b6c00063-961c-57de-9996-aa7c34c1b08f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340780} {:answer/id #uuid "ff350533-a65b-5d8c-9636-be091780422d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341376}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff350533-a65b-5d8c-9636-be091780422d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341376} :answer-field/id #uuid "90108bbe-e3f9-552f-9a17-4a20b275ed27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341634}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "40af3b43-8968-5b55-af69-0a7608e19327" :question/math-academy-id "q-72838" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{4}{7}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to one decimal place?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{4}{7})=0.962\\,550…≈0.962\\,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0.962\\,6$ lies inside the specified domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=0.962\\,6.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.962\\,6.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.962\\,6.$\n\n![](images/b2/b2cd7e76526632d8507ba484246a560829882e7f10d9814f01593f7a48d9a2ac.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -0.962\\,6 \\\\  & =5.320\\,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{x}_{2}≈0.962\\,6\\cdot 5.320\\,6≈5.1\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341871 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064262 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c234eb8-4a5b-52e1-8854-c183b68184bd" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Position of a Particle Given Its Velocity and Initial Position"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05d966cc-8fed-5585-9802-da0009c421f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}-\\frac{63}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339178} {:answer/id #uuid "7a5bdb47-642c-5847-b415-7acd09462b3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340221} {:answer/id #uuid "ab6c10e8-5890-5117-ba59-b6c73fa45d62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{4}-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340689} {:answer/id #uuid "e28cd841-ca6d-595a-825e-c4c1810fa0a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341135} {:answer/id #uuid "f7f93847-8a6b-5df2-9c42-ff3305643c26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}+\\frac{7}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341320}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7f93847-8a6b-5df2-9c42-ff3305643c26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}+\\frac{7}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341320} :answer-field/id #uuid "741f9a31-4ea0-5b35-9dc9-dd1dfde7fb98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341584}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "35ab132d-9e7c-5e1c-baf1-f58667f680d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-7284" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t)=\\frac{1}{4}t-{t}^{2},$ where $t>0$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x=-7$ when $t=3,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{1}{4}t-{t}^{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $x(3)=-7.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7 & =\\frac{1}{8}(3)^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}(3)^{3}+C \\\\ -7 & =-\\frac{63}{8}+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{7}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}+\\frac{7}{8}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341876 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092164 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9f482ac-9771-52a3-9517-8494c3de6379" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cosine with Quadrantal Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48a7f416-9ccc-595c-937e-87b4c13463c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={30}^{∘},\\,{210}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339775} {:answer/id #uuid "6d4e1aa1-9741-5265-a620-2ea9b913f9f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={0}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340094} {:answer/id #uuid "aebf971f-72a5-59d6-b10a-ed17ef40c94d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={180}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340722} {:answer/id #uuid "d9cf140c-f381-5614-bcb7-c52362b8a27c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={60}^{∘},\\,{240}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341061} {:answer/id #uuid "e6edd2e5-fda5-5b71-a1c5-87184ab67800" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341170}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aebf971f-72a5-59d6-b10a-ed17ef40c94d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={180}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340722} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce966d3f-597e-52d1-908c-5971b6d20f49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341742}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3dee65bc-3bdf-57d3-9d7b-8a1802789c99" :question/math-academy-id "q-72845" :question/problem "Find the solution(s) to the equation $\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3}{2}=1$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3 & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the solutions to $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1$ correspond to the values of $x$ at which $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ attains its minimum value.\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-1)={180}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${180}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={180}^{∘}.$\n\nThis solution is a quadrantal angle. To find the remaining solutions, we use our knowledge of the cosine function. We know that the cosine function has its minimum values at\n\n$$\nx={180}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n.\n$$\n\nSince the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}$ is one period, and we have already found one minimum value in this period, we conclude that $x={180}^{∘}$ is the only solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341878 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059773 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "691b9107-a935-5965-b3ac-0f3a193ce9bd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Appropriate Row Operation To Transform One Matrix Into Another"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14beb007-c69a-5ab7-974c-8b10dc220da9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339312} {:answer/id #uuid "195eaddc-f871-5643-a440-887c3d944ede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339352} {:answer/id #uuid "5638c8ca-be0f-53bb-8e47-8a06ac66a0a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339916} {:answer/id #uuid "a87e9a85-4c7f-5663-9658-9578bf406123" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340659} {:answer/id #uuid "dd65c7d4-9bf4-5a81-bb2c-8537f1f6b07f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341094}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a87e9a85-4c7f-5663-9658-9578bf406123" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340659} :answer-field/id #uuid "327a5532-0a35-5d86-90b5-6ede866a293a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341471}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4a88dee0-a622-519b-8c88-19f1fcfff46b" :question/math-academy-id "q-72854" :question/problem "Which of the following elementary row operations transforms the matrix $X$ into the matrix $W$, where\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,W=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 6\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $1$st and the $3$rd rows are the same in both matrices. The only row that has changed is the $2$nd row. \n\nBy considering the given row operations that modify the $2$nd row, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =W.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341881 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059774 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0464b51e-8568-521e-96a5-a65c04569c2a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Appropriate Row Operation To Transform a Given Entry to a Desired Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44ff5f78-99ee-52f3-bd1d-6f04102d7f6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339741} {:answer/id #uuid "64bc7a5d-f05c-508b-8e6f-0c816e66dd68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{3}↔{R}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340019} {:answer/id #uuid "66d5039f-7e91-5441-b9ac-066a1add60d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{3}↔{R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340032} {:answer/id #uuid "6efcee92-7f3a-5b03-8419-b8d8e7b73d4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-2{R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340112} {:answer/id #uuid "98eddd1d-580c-5f43-aabf-9b49e45c9fcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340508}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44ff5f78-99ee-52f3-bd1d-6f04102d7f6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339741} :answer-field/id #uuid "51eb3628-ef5f-5e6a-a72e-2dd27e5eda70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341527}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d64eac74-31dd-5514-b904-fdc9f48abd1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-72860" :question/problem "Given that\n$S=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]$,\nwhich of the following elementary row operations makes ${a}_{33}=0?$" :question/worked-solution "Of the given options, we consider the row operation \n\n$$\n{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1}={R}_{3}+(-3)\\cdot {R}_{1}\n$$\n\nApplying the above row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3)\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is \n\n$$\n{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3)\\cdot {R}_{1}\n$$\n\nor simply\n\n$$\n{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403599 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087724 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6381faa4-5dfd-58fa-ace7-411fd2a97e98" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the x-Intercepts of an Ellipse By Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef8edbea-4da2-562c-be95-1803e0e50af8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403601} {:answer/id #uuid "e45d242e-ec58-51d9-b98e-0c301509d596" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(10\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403602} {:answer/id #uuid "9141967a-e95f-5916-97f3-21bd825b34c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is no $x$-intercept" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403603} {:answer/id #uuid "3903afea-29b9-5179-b7c5-dc45513572f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403604} {:answer/id #uuid "88c9f761-6944-512d-b426-301b6d2b5ff0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(10\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403605}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef8edbea-4da2-562c-be95-1803e0e50af8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403601} :answer-field/id #uuid "629087fb-db7f-51a8-827b-0862fb389bb9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403600}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3538d2b4-fd15-5d04-9456-067812f7d246" :question/math-academy-id "q-72913" :question/problem "Calculate the points where the ellipse $2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-20x+6y+47=0$ intercepts the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercepts, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-20x+6y+47 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+3(0)^{2}-20x+6(0)+47 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-20x+47 & =0 \\\\ 2[{x}^{2}-10x]+47 & =0 \\\\ 2[(x-5)^{2}-25]+47 & =0 \\\\ 2(x-5)^{2}-50+47 & =0 \\\\ 2(x-5)^{2} & =3 \\\\ (x-5)^{2} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ x-5 & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}} \\\\ x & =5\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at $(5-\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}},0)$ and $(5+\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}},0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341886 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087724 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6381faa4-5dfd-58fa-ace7-411fd2a97e98" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the x-Intercepts of an Ellipse By Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bedf0ef-d091-529c-9452-055d4df48089" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\pm \\sqrt{3},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339962} {:answer/id #uuid "7b290d59-ce77-5ad9-b904-8c21b160ec07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340231} {:answer/id #uuid "907bfe3d-4729-5dfa-8728-e4a9413ce8e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2\\pm \\sqrt{3},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340423} {:answer/id #uuid "d409ed3f-53f8-5d27-b84d-7ce80fe544a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341002} {:answer/id #uuid "ea330265-17ea-5ce6-9db7-de0ab2bee739" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is no $x$-intercept" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bedf0ef-d091-529c-9452-055d4df48089" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\pm \\sqrt{3},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339962} :answer-field/id #uuid "98d12b9f-78ac-5e61-9bc9-9638586d0302" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341648}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0e89bb93-f1e6-5d54-9eb6-f01d87f935ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-72914" :question/problem "Calculate the points where the ellipse ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4x-2y+1=0$ intercepts the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercepts, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4x-2y+1 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{0}^{2}-4x-2(0)+1 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+1 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}-4+1 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =3 \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{3} \\\\ x & =2\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at $(2-\\sqrt{3},0)$ and $(2+\\sqrt{3},0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403606 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087726 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d21d6066-f7c8-5a47-b150-d0ae25d2d60b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Center and Axis Lengths Given an Inhomogeneous Polynomial Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c381e28-47d1-516c-ba70-7941c1b4c573" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403608} {:answer/id #uuid "c7ab0ed2-8c80-5ee7-ad45-3c43ece3b795" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403609} {:answer/id #uuid "e0ac736d-0463-57d0-9db1-2a7177ddc558" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403610} {:answer/id #uuid "b2703eaa-98a6-5759-b55c-68a1aa801db2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403611} {:answer/id #uuid "cfd845e5-ca89-50e3-8d11-42ad65bcd599" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403612}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2703eaa-98a6-5759-b55c-68a1aa801db2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403611} :answer-field/id #uuid "66032e6a-59fa-5bca-a1f1-2d6e69a901e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403607}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a0bc6070-452c-59d7-b850-234ba4c8bcd8" :question/math-academy-id "q-72934" :question/problem "Determine  the lengths of the major and minor axes of the ellipse whose equation is ${x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-4x+16y=-16.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-4x+16y & =-16 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-4x)+(4{y}^{2}+16y) & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must complete the square for both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-4x)+(4{y}^{2}+16y) & =-16 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-4x)+4({y}^{2}+4y) & =-16 \\\\ [(x-2)^{2}-4]+4[(y+2)^{2}-4] & =-16 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}-4+4(y+2)^{2}-16 & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}-4+4(y+2)^{2}-16 & =-16 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+4(y+2)^{2} & =-16+16+4 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+4(y+2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{1} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{{1}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2>1,$ the length of the major axis is $2\\cdot 2=4,$ and the length of the minor axis is $2\\cdot 1=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341896 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096804 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0a16e7b6-2a09-5aeb-9a8b-ea8fb1d59807" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Function From Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "062499b4-be47-5b28-982d-86c642bba935" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339180} {:answer/id #uuid "0c2e9142-707f-5bcc-a580-ee524bee6fdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339234} {:answer/id #uuid "359a8a35-2225-5f23-8b9d-aae7b68bb0a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339604} {:answer/id #uuid "96928fb6-9fc9-5f9b-b89c-531b735c25b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340487} {:answer/id #uuid "c6587bc5-7cad-5be9-a21b-564e512cd0a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340885}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96928fb6-9fc9-5f9b-b89c-531b735c25b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340487} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f07086c-8b55-5351-9a76-5d3e1c547bcf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341518}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "241c4369-248c-546e-84df-6e2b47d7a0e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-72998" :question/problem "![](images/6d/6d5146afb9508532145180a4f632c0b5c4495487b0a9a89cfd4f71e134b95f50.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is given above. Find ${f}^{′}(-1.5).$" :question/worked-solution "To compute ${f}^{′}(-1.5),$ we need to find the slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ at $x=-1.5.$\n\nLet's consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ on $[-2,1].$ Notice that $x=-1.5$ lies in this interval.\n\n![](images/22/2216d3014a8c74102d5e298c300092a575798de0b66d89914716898bc16ac655.png)\n\nThe slope of $y=f(x)$ on $x\\in (-2,1)$ is constant and equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{0-2}{1-(-2)}=-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(-1.5)=-\\frac{2}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341897 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119956 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "231604dc-fe8f-59be-90fe-37af7a1bb648" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying When a Rational Function has no Vertical Asymptotes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "376f085b-77ed-5e18-bbad-6721aae3af84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339626} {:answer/id #uuid "b91a65c8-9a0b-56ec-9d6d-f87b5d22dd2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340788} {:answer/id #uuid "c749d57a-0aa3-57ce-847c-3cb03b25ce49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340895} {:answer/id #uuid "e993d552-3194-5781-abb3-16538d649d32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$, $x=-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341190} {:answer/id #uuid "fdb3a956-1ef1-5c3b-89cd-d12677010c7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c749d57a-0aa3-57ce-847c-3cb03b25ce49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340895} :answer-field/id #uuid "6802aeef-3bad-5c37-81c1-c577e7e557d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341560}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f02481a9-b93c-590a-b202-053a18900f95" :question/math-academy-id "q-73015" :question/problem "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}}{2x+4}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's simplify the expression by factoring both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}}{2x+4} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}(x+2)}{2(x+2)}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the denominator is never zero, we conclude that this function has no vertical asymptotes. A plot of the function is below. Note that it is not defined at $x=-2.$\n\n![](images/3a/3ad83a79ccd47d9d035ac5a3f47b929a1e6b3939cdaddee9ff0f2d6a3cf07e56.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234328 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179659 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "44e3a595-a9ae-5d8c-9aa5-c8e3403fcb28" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying a Variable Expression Containing a Quotient of Factorials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ae24c67-169d-568f-9906-74f5b3cc2e0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n(n+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339505} {:answer/id #uuid "35c068a3-dbe1-516f-a719-8c52f792f017" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{n}^{2}(n+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339606} {:answer/id #uuid "4cc01694-bcd6-5105-860b-e7ae3171d223" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n(n-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339824} {:answer/id #uuid "9d31eea3-a5b0-5f91-81ac-06d7e2e82a81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(n-1)(n+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340545} {:answer/id #uuid "cb8da782-b040-553f-a209-d1c9c3875b8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n(n-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340932}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ae24c67-169d-568f-9906-74f5b3cc2e0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n(n+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339505} :answer-field/id #uuid "20784676-1346-5beb-bf37-2d8560fbcb5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341437}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "81d120ad-3169-5d64-9a97-a76dfeab46ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-73023" :question/problem "Given that $n\\ge 1$ is an integer, simplify $\\frac{(n+1)!}{(n-1)!}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice the following:\n\n$$\n(n+1)!=(n+1)\\cdot n\\cdot (n-1)!\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel the $(n-1)!$ that occurs in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(n+1)!}{(n-1)!} & =\\frac{(n+1)\\cdot n\\cdot (n-1)!}{(n-1)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{(n+1)\\cdot n\\cdot \\cancel{(n-1)!}}{\\cancel{(n-1)!}} \\\\  & =n(n+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234331 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179659 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "44e3a595-a9ae-5d8c-9aa5-c8e3403fcb28" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying a Variable Expression Containing a Quotient of Factorials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29487ed3-a525-52dd-9167-d626037dc815" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{n+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339491} {:answer/id #uuid "7f774ed2-5f28-52ae-865d-32a61cabb3ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{n+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340273} {:answer/id #uuid "9c213996-40cb-543e-9a55-403ef758636c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340533} {:answer/id #uuid "9d2f4e0c-cc16-5245-84c3-7f919dd4a20d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}+n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340543} {:answer/id #uuid "dca1f91a-c517-5f9d-abf6-091011b8edcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341087}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29487ed3-a525-52dd-9167-d626037dc815" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{n+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339491} :answer-field/id #uuid "247d41fd-6e73-5f0c-95b3-a198cf2975b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341444}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "72330c15-2550-5193-88bf-8afccb0b16d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-73028" :question/problem "Given that $n\\ge -1$ is an integer, simplify $\\frac{(n+1)!}{(n+2)!}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice the following:\n\n$$\n(n+2)!=(n+2)\\cdot (n+1)!\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel the $(n+1)!$ that occurs in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(n+1)!}{(n+2)!} & =\\frac{(n+1)!}{(n+2)\\cdot (n+1)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(n+1)!}}{(n+2)\\cdot \\cancel{(n+1)!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341899 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119957 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "68ba4c5d-8b91-57bc-b98b-0e9f8d8f1f67" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Vertical Asymptotes for the Sum of Two Rational Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52a883e7-4d2a-532d-9bbd-dce6962edcc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339890} {:answer/id #uuid "5a4e1d9b-7c6a-5c7d-b288-86ddd171b3c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$, $x=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339949} {:answer/id #uuid "89cc96ba-2754-55f6-8b86-473c4e42f001" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=\\frac{1}{2}$, $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340358} {:answer/id #uuid "8f1ac77d-4b8a-5f0e-932d-f01c276b93ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340415} {:answer/id #uuid "a9c87c4a-a6d6-5db3-8002-3e4994f240d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340674}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a4e1d9b-7c6a-5c7d-b288-86ddd171b3c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$, $x=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339949} :answer-field/id #uuid "929e3387-fa4c-54fe-ab80-91ba52610238" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341637}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d13dc12b-246a-57c9-9dc3-693e68fcfaf7" :question/math-academy-id "q-73031" :question/problem "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x-1}+\\frac{1}{x+1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's put the two terms over a common denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x-1}+\\frac{1}{x+1} & =\\frac{x+1+(x-1)}{(x-1)(x+1)}=\\frac{2x}{(x-1)(x+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we proceed to set the denominator equal to zero and get\n\n$$\n(x-1)(x+1)=0\\,⟹\\,x=1\\,,\\,x=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertical asymptotes are at $x=1$ and $x=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234332 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179660 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "644b7f09-7f66-50f2-8eba-ef6e009429d0" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying a Variable Expression Containing a Quotient With Many Factorials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04802008-4ed6-5b73-83f5-d29dc06587f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339160} {:answer/id #uuid "1ba86355-0ac6-5962-ae05-e44d9250bd1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{k}^{2}+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339366} {:answer/id #uuid "af54b886-24b1-56d3-9d8e-4c71dd5f011f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{k+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340727} {:answer/id #uuid "f4bcde05-f467-51e7-b960-da690faabda6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{k}{k+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341287} {:answer/id #uuid "f7792b0b-5d92-53fc-957c-3f59a201b930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{k}^{2}+2k}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341316}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af54b886-24b1-56d3-9d8e-4c71dd5f011f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{k+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340727} :answer-field/id #uuid "e83b7853-94c1-56fb-bb60-7f7706a1acaa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341792}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4c27af90-5244-552c-a6c5-2d976ec6599f" :question/math-academy-id "q-73037" :question/problem "Given that $k\\ge -1$ is an integer, $\\frac{(k+2)((k+1)!)^{2}}{((k+2)!)^{2}}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we notice that\n\n$$\n(k+2)!=(k+2)(k+1)!\n$$\n\nThen, we can cancel the $k!$ that occurs in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(k+2)((k+1)!)^{2}}{((k+2)!)^{2}} & =\\frac{(k+2)\\cdot (k+1)!\\cdot (k+1)!}{(k+2)!\\cdot (k+2)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{(k+2)\\cdot (k+1)!\\cdot (k+1)!}{(k+2)\\cdot (k+1)!\\cdot (k+2)\\cdot (k+1)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(k+2)}\\cdot \\cancel{(k+1)!}\\cdot \\cancel{(k+1)!}}{(k+2)\\cdot \\cancel{(k+1)!}\\cdot \\cancel{(k+2)}\\cdot \\cancel{(k+1)!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{k+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
